WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.490
I don't know if we need,
if we want to do this, but—

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:05.160
I already look like that naturally, so.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:07.050
Oh, this is Kevin from "The Office."

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:08.360
Yeah, yeah.

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:11.610
Eric Glyman is the
co-founder and CEO of Ramp,

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:13.920
the corporate card and
finance automation platform.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.692
Founded in 2019, Ramp has
taken the industry by storm.

00:00:17.692 --> 00:00:18.525
Oh, cheers.

00:00:18.525 --> 00:00:19.358
Cheers.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.860
Can you describe maybe a good framing,

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:24.480
just the Ramp business today?

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:25.830
What's the biggest part of the business

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:27.120
in terms of where you make money?

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:28.800
What are the new growth lines?

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.180
Any metrics you can share on the scale?

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:32.100
Alex and I have a million questions,

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:34.500
but maybe you can start by
just framing it up for us.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:35.403
Yeah, of course.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:41.460
So first, just the pace
that this has come together

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:42.997
has been pretty remarkable.

00:00:42.997 --> 00:00:43.830
Seven years, yeah.

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:44.663
You know?

00:00:44.663 --> 00:00:48.450
I think by the time we
were, six years and change,

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:51.060
the company had passed over
a billion a year in revenue.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:54.363
The largest portion of that is card.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:56.760
And so, that might be a classic

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.220
kind of interchange-based model.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:00.053
But behind it—

00:01:00.053 --> 00:01:00.886
So this is people,

00:01:00.886 --> 00:01:02.010
in their business they have spend cards,

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:03.613
everyone walking around the
company has a Ramp card.

00:01:03.613 --> 00:01:05.460
And you run interchange on those.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:07.740
That's right.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:11.823
Next, you can think about
bill payments and software.

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:14.700
Software, it's a

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.100
two-something-year-old business line,

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.380
just about two years and some months.

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:20.520
That's over 100 million a year

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:21.930
business line in and of itself.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:25.380
And that can be advanced
functionality to maybe manage

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:28.050
lots of entities, to automate
aspects of accounting,

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:31.380
maybe aspects of
procurement, bill payments.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:34.710
So this can be sending checks, wires, ACH

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:36.600
and that's predominantly a float

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.960
as well in some cases foreign
exchange transaction business.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:41.520
Treasury.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.800
That's a product that is about a year old,

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.330
several billion dollars of deposits.

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:47.070
Some of that is checking-like products.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:49.290
Some of that is more of an investment

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:52.410
and money ladder type product.

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:55.590
And then the last, the
other ones are procurement

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.050
and then travel, which
is a bit of an in-kind.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:00.480
And what's been so interesting
is that if you break down

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.930
and look towards maybe let's
say contribution profit,

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:06.030
gross profit of the business,

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:08.280
a few years ago it would've been

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.500
90 plus percent card.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.590
I think by the end of
this year, the second,

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:15.870
third, fourth, fifth lines of business

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:18.060
will comprise and aggregate

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.277
the majority of Ramp's business.

00:02:20.277 --> 00:02:24.060
And so, it's evolved into
this platform by which

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.790
if you're trying to operate your company,

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:29.578
it's just a lot more efficient.

00:02:29.578 --> 00:02:33.990
You spend less. I think
people know Ramp for,

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:35.910
we help the average
company cut their expenses

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:37.950
by about 5% per year.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.290
The thing that's been really fascinating

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:42.990
is people are starting to use
these products in aggregate.

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:43.980
Not only are they not paying

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:46.770
for five, six sets of point solutions,

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:48.450
but they're also not wasting time.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:49.290
They're growing faster.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.840
The average customer last year on Ramp

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.840
grew their revenue by, I think it was 16%,

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:59.370
which compared to, in the United States,

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:00.203
the average business—

00:03:00.203 --> 00:03:01.036
Much higher than that.

00:03:01.036 --> 00:03:01.869
Yeah, I think it's 5% in the US.

00:03:01.869 --> 00:03:05.160
And so, in some sense
what we're trying to do

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:08.250
is be not just a better platform,

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:12.420
but a better, almost kind of
digital brain for organizations

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:15.540
to allocate resources and
make sure spend is not wasted.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:16.770
Yeah.

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:21.030
So, you start by selling,

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:23.280
say, spend cards into a business.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.130
The CFO likes it, the employees like it,

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:27.960
and then that gives you
permission to go sell

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.310
some of this other functionality.

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:32.400
And so it's kind of a classic
multi-product cross sell.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:34.320
Is it always starting with spend cards

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.330
or do you now have multiple front doors

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.260
into the business?

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:39.120
It's been the big story of the past year.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:40.590
I think there were,

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:41.423
over the last quarter,

00:03:41.423 --> 00:03:45.780
thousands of businesses that
came in just for bill payments.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:50.760
There's accounting firms
that are becoming into,

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:52.140
they say, "We're a Ramp shop here

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:54.300
if you want to work with us.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:55.620
We'll bring in bill payments,

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:58.500
treasury..."

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:00.000
We use Ramp to go power that

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.380
and they can manage 10, 100, 200 clients

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:05.630
all through one platform.

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:07.950
And we're excited.

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:10.380
I mean, probably the fastest growing line

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:12.420
of business is procurement.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:13.920
Or, they're single
clients that are looking

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.650
to kind of bring in and do full-scale

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:18.183
purchase orders across,

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:20.220
whether it's single

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:23.070
or a dozen plus entities,
all the way through.

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:24.870
And so, that's been

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:27.540
the really fun story of the past year.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:30.690
Do you have a view on
what the right policy is

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:32.910
for employee spend within a business?

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:34.140
I love that you ask this, yeah.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.847
37signals or someone will say,

00:04:36.847 --> 00:04:38.010
"Companies are so lame.

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:39.270
you just give everyone a credit card

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:40.890
and trust people and it's fine."

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:43.087
And then meanwhile,
large companies are like,

00:04:43.087 --> 00:04:46.200
"Well, this hotel is in a Tier 2 city

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:47.520
and therefore there's this dollar limit

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:49.710
and it must be booked 14 days in advance."

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:51.840
And so, you see companies
operate at really

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:53.550
two ends of the spectrum in terms of

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:55.260
how much flexibility they give people.

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:57.930
What is correct for a company,
or does it just vary by size?

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.150
I love this question.

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:04.110
And the interesting part,
if you really kind of

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:05.910
dig into Ramp's businesses

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:07.680
we have ways of back testing

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:08.980
and actually understanding

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:13.110
based on how strict your policy is,

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:14.370
how things are running.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:17.430
Does that have an impact on how much time

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:20.100
your employees are,
let's say doing expenses,

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:22.890
how quickly you're growing.

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:24.750
And you can start to actually compare

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:27.840
based off of the operating
hygiene of the company…

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.300
What are end margins?

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:33.840
What is a pace of growth
that occurs at the company?

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:34.980
And I bring this back to what—

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:36.570
So you can do a correlational study

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:38.236
between expense policies

00:05:38.236 --> 00:05:39.069
and company growth.

00:05:39.924 --> 00:05:41.519
Yeah.

00:05:41.519 --> 00:05:43.860
And look, it's pretty interesting.

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:47.580
I mean, the first, there's an aspect of,

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:49.860
in most companies, particularly
high growth companies,

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:51.810
it tends to resemble something closer to

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:54.390
the "no rules rules" Netflix approach

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:57.840
of we're going to trust but
verify and shine a light on it.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:00.720
Spend tends to be reasonably permissive.

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:02.580
But then part of the
breakthrough of the past year

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:05.190
is you could basically
take an expense policy,

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:07.410
let's say it's written

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:09.240
in plain English.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:10.980
"If the flight is more than five hours,

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:11.813
you can take business.

00:06:11.813 --> 00:06:14.550
If it's under..." Things that
would've been horrible to go

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:18.570
and have your managers
go and verify this stuff,

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:20.940
now you can run that through an LLM.

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:24.390
I think that we're processing
over 100,000 expenses a day

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:27.033
that are being reviewed agentically.

00:06:27.876 --> 00:06:30.840
This is a very fast-growing
subset of the business.

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:33.300
So you give the Ramp agent

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:35.870
this company's expense
policy and then it applies it

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:37.380
to the transactions
that are coming through.

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:38.250
Exactly.

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:39.720
And so you can start to do things

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:43.230
like, I think about the episode
you'd had with Susan Li.

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:45.273
It was incredibly instructive where,

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:49.900
she sort of says, it would feel so silly

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:52.920
and it's not a great use
of my time in some sense

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:55.650
to be a very expensive machine
learning algorithm to do,

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:56.670
to review expenses.

00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:58.590
But yet somehow a lot
of people in companies

00:06:58.590 --> 00:07:00.630
are reduced to that
thanks to Sarbanes-Oxley

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:02.040
and the rules around it.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:04.830
Now you can have an agent
functionally that does that,

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:06.840
that takes that aspect of the job.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:09.600
And so, it has access
to the full set of data

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:12.840
that you would, all the
transaction-related metadata,

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:15.870
the receipt data, the timing
data, the policy data.

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:19.500
It then can go in real-time review.

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:20.700
Was this in or out?

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:23.310
Can have the full audit trail
explaining its reasoning.

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:25.290
And today it's over 99% accurate,

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:28.020
which turns out it's much
more accurate than people are.

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:29.580
So people don't know the expense policy

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:31.710
and expenses are pretty automated.

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:35.160
And I find this thing
super interesting, in part

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:39.090
because I don't think it
should be anyone's job

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:40.800
to go and review people's expenses,

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:43.650
but somehow, while it's in no one's JD,

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:45.960
the law kind of requires
everyone to waste an hour

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:47.460
of their time every month,
if you're a manager.

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:49.890
And does the law allow for
it to be reviewed agentically

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:51.960
and then just signed off on by the human?

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:52.793
It does, yeah.

00:07:52.793 --> 00:07:55.750
The goal of this is separation of duties.

00:07:55.750 --> 00:07:57.810
That you yourself

00:07:57.810 --> 00:07:59.700
are not reviewing your own expenses.

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:00.533
There needs to be

00:08:00.533 --> 00:08:03.420
a set of procedures that govern

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:06.810
and actually are effective
through the action of, okay,

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:08.130
the rule says this.

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:09.990
Did you take some steps to do this?

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:10.823
It can be a system—

00:08:10.823 --> 00:08:12.300
Yeah, you can't self-certify.

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:15.120
The funny thing is that, I
mean, one way of doing this…

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:16.470
Actually, we used to do this is

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:18.450
why not just post your expense reports

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:20.100
in the public eye, right?

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:22.800
So, and the reason to do this
is actually it could go wrong.

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:23.850
You could say, "I'm going to spend...

00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:24.825
You stay at the Four Seasons,

00:08:24.825 --> 00:08:26.100
I'm going stay at the Five Seasons."

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:27.570
You could go that direction.

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:30.120
But what you actually
want is a moral code.

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:30.960
It's like,

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:32.400
if you were the owner of the business

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:33.910
it is your own money.
Yes.

00:08:33.910 --> 00:08:35.760
So if you were staying
at the Five Seasons,

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:37.230
I've just made that hotel chain up,

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:38.063
it's like, you're—

00:08:38.063 --> 00:08:38.896
Sounds pretty nice.

00:08:38.896 --> 00:08:41.310
You're stealing from yourself,
why would you do that?

00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:43.260
The problem is that once you get

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:45.060
to tens of thousands of people,

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:46.860
how do you impose that moral code?

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:47.970
And actually like the two

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:51.593
tent poles that you just
mentioned, they're both bad.

00:08:51.593 --> 00:08:54.750
Because if you have to
stay at a crappy hotel

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:57.990
and you're about to sign a
two million dollar contract

00:08:57.990 --> 00:09:00.090
and you didn't get any
sleep. It's the Motel 6

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:01.410
and you take a two-hour taxi,

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:03.905
it's like, all right, you
saved the company money.

00:09:03.905 --> 00:09:04.738
That does not make sense.

00:09:04.738 --> 00:09:06.623
Stay at the Four Seasons.

00:09:06.623 --> 00:09:08.310
But you can't really embody that

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:09.557
in, "if this, then that."

00:09:09.557 --> 00:09:12.960
Because what you want is this moral code.

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:14.130
Like that's what you want.

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:16.110
And like how do you actually instill that?

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:18.270
And there's almost a
behavioral psychology way,

00:09:18.270 --> 00:09:19.410
which is like, we'll just

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:21.000
show what people are spending.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:22.650
If you're at the top of the leaderboard,

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:25.620
we might trust but verify
and verify a lot more.

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:27.060
But that's the hard thing to do

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:28.980
and actually preserve
as the company goes from

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:31.560
the founder, to the founder
plus the founder's brother

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:33.660
to many, many more people.

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:36.600
How do you keep that same esprit de corps

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:39.480
or this informal code.

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:41.523
I think it comes back to…

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:44.183
What was the name of the movie?

00:09:44.183 --> 00:09:46.740
"12 Angry Men," I think it was

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:49.470
where, as you're watching the film,

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:51.630
you get some level of detail

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:53.580
and someone seems very guilty.

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:55.170
It's a dead shut case.

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:56.550
And as more evidence emerges,

00:09:56.550 --> 00:09:59.670
you realize actually,
this may be different.

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:01.410
And you need more context

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:04.500
in order to arrive at
maybe a moral conclusion.

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:07.560
Did it relate to closing a project?

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:11.100
How does it relate to an outcome

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:13.830
that maybe you'll see
emerge one month, two months

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:15.570
after you stayed at the Five Seasons

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:17.460
and closed the deal that
changed the company,

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:18.420
whatever the example is.

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:22.530
I would argue in favor of tools

00:10:22.530 --> 00:10:26.430
that have more context,
that do more jobs of work.

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:30.420
Because not only can you
do the narrow job better,

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:31.920
but you can start to get at the answer

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:34.170
of what actually is
right for shareholders.

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:35.850
Where should we be allocating capital?

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:36.683
It's so interesting to hear

00:10:36.683 --> 00:10:40.140
because every company
approaches this differently.

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:41.640
Like I'm on the board of Wise

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:44.880
and you know, Kristo
flies coach everywhere,

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:47.370
everybody flies, that's just the policy

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:50.850
but it might be penny-wise, pound-foolish.

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:51.840
Again, not for me to say,

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:54.693
but I also really admire
the corporate code.

00:10:54.693 --> 00:10:57.090
It's just like you,
actually, that is cultural.

00:10:57.090 --> 00:10:58.050
It's kind of weird

00:10:58.050 --> 00:11:00.030
to say expenses are
cultural, but they are.

00:11:00.030 --> 00:11:00.870
For sure.

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:03.000
Yeah, it's shared—

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:03.833
Shared belief system.

00:11:03.833 --> 00:11:05.040
It's a shared belief system.

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:06.960
It's like, what is the
culture of the company?

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:10.230
You talked about your
expansion into Bill Pay.

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:13.290
It seems like bill payment

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:16.740
has proven, over the past 40 years,

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:20.190
uniquely resistant to
automation and modernization.

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:23.220
If you look at how a typical business

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:27.150
pays their rent or if you
order a keg of Guinness,

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:30.750
you'll get a PDF emailed to you

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:33.120
by some person who has
a relationship with you

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:34.680
and there will be bank account details

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:36.236
which hopefully are correct.

00:11:36.236 --> 00:11:39.300
And you send a payment to those.

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:44.044
And even bill payment products,

00:11:44.044 --> 00:11:45.900
you guys have a bunch of technology here,

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:49.200
but it's like, we scan the
PDF in an automated way

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:50.850
or we ensure that the bank account details

00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:52.050
weren't mistyped or something.

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:54.000
But it's kind of "putting
a little bit of lipstick

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:56.670
on the pig" of the whole system

00:11:56.670 --> 00:11:58.380
being super antiquated.

00:11:58.380 --> 00:11:59.910
Why is the system so antiquated

00:11:59.910 --> 00:12:02.910
when it comes to bill
payment in particular?

00:12:02.910 --> 00:12:05.100
I think this is some of
what Alex was bringing up.

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:08.490
It's the constraints of programming things

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:11.220
in an "if this, then that"
kind of world are very heavy.

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:12.540
There's a lot of complexity.

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:15.870
And when you think about the nuance

00:12:15.870 --> 00:12:18.450
and algorithms that govern

00:12:18.450 --> 00:12:21.360
why do companies spend money
under some circumstances,

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:23.163
how hard it can be to record.

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:27.000
I wouldn't overlook...

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:30.690
Let's say that you're a manufacturer

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:32.730
and you're buying some asset
which you're going to use

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:34.440
for five years and it's
going to depreciate.

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:35.910
It's a very different accounting treatment

00:12:35.910 --> 00:12:38.310
versus I'm buying a
pay-as-you-go SaaS app.

00:12:39.630 --> 00:12:41.280
All the complexities around that

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:43.710
might govern whether or
not you decide to spend.

00:12:43.710 --> 00:12:46.050
And then finally, once
you get all this detail,

00:12:46.050 --> 00:12:47.220
how do you review this

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:50.760
and decide where to allocate
your next marginal dollar

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:52.170
is very complicated.

00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:55.950
But many systems are in place upgraded

00:12:55.950 --> 00:12:57.540
despite the fact that
that's pretty complex.

00:12:57.540 --> 00:12:59.040
So credit cards started

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:01.320
with no real-time authorization system,

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:02.153
which is crazy.

00:13:02.153 --> 00:13:03.567
And you just hoped that
they were good for—

00:13:03.567 --> 00:13:04.950
Carbon copy.

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:08.250
Exactly. The machine
with the pleasing sound.

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:10.860
They'd do great in the
current ASMR environment.

00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:12.360
That's true.

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:13.590
But then they added,

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:15.690
you could call up and get an authorization

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:16.770
and then they obviously added

00:13:16.770 --> 00:13:18.120
to the current systems we know where—

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:19.260
The modem, you forgot the modem.

00:13:19.260 --> 00:13:20.790
Exactly. And then the modem.

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:24.660
And so, in place credit
cards were upgraded

00:13:24.660 --> 00:13:25.710
with much better capabilities.

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:27.030
And similarly, if you look at checks

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:28.020
and how they work,

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:30.540
even though we think of
checks as antiquated,

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:32.190
it used to be the case
that physical checks had

00:13:32.190 --> 00:13:33.660
to be flown all around the country

00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:35.903
to be physically settled.

00:13:35.903 --> 00:13:37.110
And then there was the Check 21 Act in,

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:38.250
what was it, the year 2000?

00:13:38.250 --> 00:13:41.700
Where they said a scan
of a check is good enough

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:43.830
and so they could be
digitized by the banks

00:13:43.830 --> 00:13:44.670
and then shredded.

00:13:44.670 --> 00:13:45.750
And then there was the,

00:13:45.750 --> 00:13:47.010
you can take a photo of
a check with your phone.

00:13:47.010 --> 00:13:49.380
And so we've managed to take this super

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:50.880
old-timey check system

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:52.110
and despite the fact it's super old-timey,

00:13:52.110 --> 00:13:54.720
in some ways it has actually
been meaningfully upgraded.

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:57.900
And if you looked at the bill
pay system from the outside,

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:00.090
you would say we should
have DNS for companies.

00:14:00.090 --> 00:14:01.500
So rather than a company

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:03.840
like sending you their
bank account details,

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:06.750
you should look them up in
some central clearinghouse.

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:08.790
And that way you confirm that
you're not being phished.

00:14:08.790 --> 00:14:10.110
A lot of spear-phishing
attacks work that way.

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:11.850
Although you referenced this.

00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:13.440
So there's actually an indifference point

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:14.370
that you can graph.

00:14:14.370 --> 00:14:16.710
If interest rates go up high enough,

00:14:16.710 --> 00:14:18.510
if I send you a check,

00:14:18.510 --> 00:14:20.760
which is like a paper mail check

00:14:20.760 --> 00:14:22.050
and that takes five days

00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:23.880
and hopefully the USPS goes on strike

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:26.640
for two more weeks so
I've got the postmark.

00:14:26.640 --> 00:14:29.130
I actually benefit… It's
actually cheaper for me.

00:14:29.130 --> 00:14:30.424
You legally paid, right?

00:14:30.424 --> 00:14:31.257
It's crazy.

00:14:31.257 --> 00:14:32.090
No, it's crazy.

00:14:32.090 --> 00:14:32.923
This is the problem.

00:14:32.923 --> 00:14:33.756
Like credit cards, everybody...

00:14:33.756 --> 00:14:35.970
I am in the store, I want the TV.

00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:37.110
You want to sell me the TV.

00:14:37.110 --> 00:14:39.210
We both want this done right away.

00:14:39.210 --> 00:14:41.940
And in the knuckle-crunching, ASMR,

00:14:41.940 --> 00:14:43.440
carbon-copy days,

00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:46.110
they might not have been
willing to sell that to me

00:14:46.110 --> 00:14:47.790
because they didn't know if
I was good for the money.

00:14:47.790 --> 00:14:49.410
They'd never seen me before.

00:14:49.410 --> 00:14:51.660
And the interesting thing is that

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:53.790
AR and AP are almost
an adversarial process.

00:14:53.790 --> 00:14:54.930
You mentioned this.

00:14:54.930 --> 00:14:57.240
What do you want to do as a controller?

00:14:57.240 --> 00:14:59.340
Well, you want to pay as late as possible

00:14:59.340 --> 00:15:01.410
and you want to collect
as early as possible.

00:15:01.410 --> 00:15:04.410
And then you have this
weird thing where checks,

00:15:04.410 --> 00:15:06.360
it's not just an accident

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:08.220
that they stick around for a long time.

00:15:08.220 --> 00:15:10.980
It's like there's a financial
incentive for one party.

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:14.190
So that's true on payment
timing, I agree with that

00:15:14.190 --> 00:15:16.260
but there are some things that
are just a dead weight loss,

00:15:16.260 --> 00:15:20.475
like the ability to fat-finger a bank account.

00:15:20.475 --> 00:15:21.480
No one benefits from that.

00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:22.644
Everyone has a horrible time

00:15:22.644 --> 00:15:23.580
if that happens.
No, that Nigerian guy.

00:15:23.580 --> 00:15:25.110
There's some people that benefit.

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:26.787
Nigerian princes.

00:15:26.787 --> 00:15:28.097
Let's think of everyone.

00:15:28.097 --> 00:15:30.450
I will posit that many networks

00:15:30.450 --> 00:15:32.010
have been upgraded in place.

00:15:32.010 --> 00:15:34.290
But somehow the loose
network of businesses

00:15:34.290 --> 00:15:37.590
paying each other via PDF invoices

00:15:37.590 --> 00:15:40.440
has proven very resistant
to in-place upgrades.

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:43.230
Also, I love your notion of DNS

00:15:43.230 --> 00:15:45.000
for payments or for expenses.

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:46.530
So, I don't know if you saw this,

00:15:46.530 --> 00:15:49.560
Google wanted to issue a 100-year bond.

00:15:49.560 --> 00:15:50.730
Yes.

00:15:50.730 --> 00:15:51.810
So, why is that interesting?

00:15:51.810 --> 00:15:54.360
Imagine that I am owed money by Google.

00:15:54.360 --> 00:15:56.760
I am one of the small
businesses that you reference.

00:15:56.760 --> 00:15:59.070
Their job is to pay me
as late as possible.

00:15:59.070 --> 00:16:00.030
Yes.

00:16:00.030 --> 00:16:02.250
They can borrow money for a hundred years

00:16:02.250 --> 00:16:04.710
at like 3% or whatever it is,

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:06.360
cheaper than the US government probably.

00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:07.193
T plus a hundred, yeah.

00:16:07.193 --> 00:16:09.150
I mean it's a little more expensive

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:10.860
than the US government,
but they are borrowing...

00:16:10.860 --> 00:16:12.210
That's crazy.

00:16:13.110 --> 00:16:15.720
I can borrow money at 20%,

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:17.100
but why do I need to borrow money?

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:19.140
Because Google's paying me late.

00:16:19.140 --> 00:16:20.700
Those jerks.

00:16:20.700 --> 00:16:22.710
So, I should be able to borrow money

00:16:22.710 --> 00:16:26.190
kind of at the rate at which
Google is borrowing money

00:16:26.190 --> 00:16:28.700
because my AR...

00:16:28.700 --> 00:16:30.420
My AR is their AP.

00:16:30.420 --> 00:16:32.970
But the problem is, unless
you put all this together,

00:16:32.970 --> 00:16:34.380
you would just look at me and you're like,

00:16:34.380 --> 00:16:35.520
"Well, you're just a little schmuck.

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:37.320
I'm going to charge you 18%."

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:39.833
It's like, yeah, but it's Google.

00:16:39.833 --> 00:16:40.920
They could borrow money at T plus

00:16:40.920 --> 00:16:43.080
whatever, you know, SOFR plus a hundred.

00:16:43.080 --> 00:16:44.580
100%.

00:16:44.580 --> 00:16:45.780
That's not fair.

00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:47.550
But the only way to really solve that

00:16:47.550 --> 00:16:49.440
is with more data and
actually entangling...

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:50.580
Not entangling, but just kind of

00:16:50.580 --> 00:16:51.870
connecting these things together.

00:16:51.870 --> 00:16:52.703
For sure.

00:16:52.703 --> 00:16:53.640
What's your vision for

00:16:54.750 --> 00:16:56.940
once AI is doing a lot
of software engineering,

00:16:56.940 --> 00:17:00.723
what does that look like in
three or four years time?

00:17:00.723 --> 00:17:03.360
Is the competitive equilibrium similar,

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:05.620
but the expectations for

00:17:07.590 --> 00:17:09.573
the amount of software and
functionality businesses

00:17:09.573 --> 00:17:12.390
are shipping is much higher?

00:17:12.390 --> 00:17:15.480
Are software teams similar
looking, different looking?

00:17:15.480 --> 00:17:18.510
Again, you're doing a
lot of AI engineering.

00:17:18.510 --> 00:17:19.800
What does the future hold?

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:21.300
It's all blurring.

00:17:21.300 --> 00:17:22.495
It's totally crazy.

00:17:22.495 --> 00:17:27.495
There's a designer shipping code.

00:17:28.290 --> 00:17:30.990
Marketing at Ramp reports into

00:17:30.990 --> 00:17:34.110
our CTO Karim, my co-founder

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:36.480
who's doing some of
like the best marketing

00:17:36.480 --> 00:17:38.010
I've ever experienced and seen.

00:17:38.010 --> 00:17:39.450
We have customer support agents

00:17:39.450 --> 00:17:41.430
like shipping code to production too.

00:17:41.430 --> 00:17:46.430
I just think that the
half-life of you see a problem

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:49.320
to how long it takes for you to go fix it

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:52.860
and do something about it
is shrinking immensely.

00:17:52.860 --> 00:17:54.360
Or if you want to change
a color of a button,

00:17:54.360 --> 00:17:57.240
you can just say, "Hey at Ramp Inspect,

00:17:57.240 --> 00:17:59.400
can you change the color of this button?"

00:17:59.400 --> 00:18:01.257
And it goes and spins it up

00:18:01.257 --> 00:18:02.820
and it does it within minutes.

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:04.440
It verifies and validates it.

00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:07.140
And so, I actually think in some sense,

00:18:07.140 --> 00:18:09.480
these classic barriers

00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:11.730
that software businesses had

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:14.100
are clearly going to erode.

00:18:14.100 --> 00:18:16.530
Yes but, there's the old joke of

00:18:16.530 --> 00:18:18.870
"It's much more fun to
write code than read code,"

00:18:18.870 --> 00:18:21.480
which explains a lot of
software engineer's behavior.

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:23.610
And it becomes easier
to change the button.

00:18:23.610 --> 00:18:26.220
Is that another instance
of it being more fun

00:18:26.220 --> 00:18:27.450
to write code than read code?

00:18:27.450 --> 00:18:31.500
Where lines of code are a
liability and not an asset

00:18:31.500 --> 00:18:32.580
because they're something

00:18:32.580 --> 00:18:33.413
you have to reckon with.

00:18:33.413 --> 00:18:36.990
And so, are companies
at some level incurring

00:18:36.990 --> 00:18:39.990
some tech debt now where
they're adding a bunch of code,

00:18:39.990 --> 00:18:42.690
which will maybe be harder
to reason with later?

00:18:42.690 --> 00:18:44.100
Or do you just get bailed out by

00:18:44.100 --> 00:18:46.650
the models getting better?

00:18:46.650 --> 00:18:47.483
It's really interesting.

00:18:47.483 --> 00:18:49.440
And by the way, one of
the other interesting

00:18:49.440 --> 00:18:52.923
sub-conversations I'm hearing a lot, too, is

00:18:52.923 --> 00:18:57.150
when you think about where
tech debt comes from,

00:18:57.150 --> 00:18:58.260
there's a set of conditions

00:18:58.260 --> 00:18:59.093
and trade-offs you make,

00:18:59.093 --> 00:19:01.830
you write things in a discreet

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:03.270
and deterministic way.

00:19:03.270 --> 00:19:05.610
Under these circumstances,
follow this code path.

00:19:05.610 --> 00:19:07.770
Under this circumstance,
follow this other one.

00:19:07.770 --> 00:19:09.210
In a world where there's LLMs

00:19:09.210 --> 00:19:11.280
and the models
themselves are improving,

00:19:11.280 --> 00:19:13.830
I think it's entirely
possible that the way

00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:15.150
that code is written is you say,

00:19:15.150 --> 00:19:17.100
"Here's what I'm solving for. Under

00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:19.380
these kinds of conditions,
here's what I want to occur.

00:19:19.380 --> 00:19:23.280
Go write the code that
drives this outcome."

00:19:23.280 --> 00:19:27.030
And maybe with the models
such as they are today

00:19:27.030 --> 00:19:28.920
can get it done in sort of a

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:30.960
spaghetti code written fashion.

00:19:30.960 --> 00:19:32.520
But it kind of works even
though it's some kind of

00:19:32.520 --> 00:19:35.040
Rube Goldberg machine underneath the hood.

00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:37.530
But as these models get much smarter,

00:19:37.530 --> 00:19:39.750
you might write your code base
in such a way where you say,

00:19:39.750 --> 00:19:43.110
"Every year, rewrite the underlying code,

00:19:43.110 --> 00:19:44.580
but here's the outcome I can drive."

00:19:44.580 --> 00:19:47.580
And today you accomplish
my outcome 90% of the time,

00:19:47.580 --> 00:19:49.063
95, 98, 99, 100.

00:19:49.063 --> 00:19:51.180
And do you just have kind of self-healing

00:19:51.180 --> 00:19:53.160
and writing code all the way through?

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:55.950
And this notion of like
underlying code does goes away

00:19:55.950 --> 00:19:58.710
because we're writing things
in this different manner.

00:19:58.710 --> 00:20:01.110
Obviously, there's real
conditions in which

00:20:01.110 --> 00:20:02.820
that's not going to… That won't occur.

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:05.167
Like, if you're writing
code that needs to have,

00:20:05.167 --> 00:20:08.550
four nines of accuracy and uptime,

00:20:08.550 --> 00:20:11.340
like that's probably not
the methods you're using.

00:20:11.340 --> 00:20:14.760
But if you're like a
growth engineering team,

00:20:14.760 --> 00:20:16.500
that's probably what you're doing today

00:20:16.500 --> 00:20:18.480
if you're kind of on the
leading edge of this stuff.

00:20:18.480 --> 00:20:23.480
And so, I find this
stuff very fascinating.

00:20:23.490 --> 00:20:24.450
And when I think about

00:20:24.450 --> 00:20:26.850
the deeper implications of this stuff,

00:20:26.850 --> 00:20:31.740
I think that if you are
completing this small amount

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:33.030
of cognitive work...

00:20:33.030 --> 00:20:36.330
Let's say you are an expense app.

00:20:36.330 --> 00:20:39.270
The spend has occurred,

00:20:39.270 --> 00:20:40.970
it needs to go get the...

00:20:41.910 --> 00:20:44.160
Write it down somewhere
and get someone's approval.

00:20:44.160 --> 00:20:46.770
And that's all the knowledge
work that you're doing.

00:20:46.770 --> 00:20:49.110
That's very few tokens in
order to accomplish that,

00:20:49.110 --> 00:20:51.240
both to create the infrastructure
in order to facilitate it,

00:20:51.240 --> 00:20:54.150
like that probably evaporates.

00:20:54.150 --> 00:20:56.580
Anyone can probably custom
write that kind of app.

00:20:56.580 --> 00:20:59.640
Whereas if you're doing
much deeper kind of work,

00:20:59.640 --> 00:21:02.580
such to underwrite a company,

00:21:02.580 --> 00:21:06.660
provide financing, automate
areas of accounting.

00:21:06.660 --> 00:21:09.210
I think the fitness function for companies

00:21:09.210 --> 00:21:11.640
becomes can you actually
do things in such a way

00:21:11.640 --> 00:21:15.120
where even if you could
spend tokens on it,

00:21:15.120 --> 00:21:16.920
it would take more tokens
to create the thing

00:21:16.920 --> 00:21:19.980
or do that work than the
system maybe that you've built

00:21:19.980 --> 00:21:22.500
to kind of drive that outcome.

00:21:22.500 --> 00:21:24.300
I just think the rules of what it means

00:21:24.300 --> 00:21:25.860
to be a software company are changing.

00:21:25.860 --> 00:21:27.810
I think network effects
and what you're building

00:21:27.810 --> 00:21:29.940
is more important than ever.

00:21:29.940 --> 00:21:32.820
Like, if you think about
the classic set of moats

00:21:32.820 --> 00:21:35.610
that are there, I think
that this question of

00:21:35.610 --> 00:21:39.630
where are their moats in a world where...

00:21:39.630 --> 00:21:41.670
I like Dario's way of putting this.

00:21:41.670 --> 00:21:43.950
If you have a country of geniuses—

00:21:43.950 --> 00:21:46.170
Data center, yeah.

00:21:46.170 --> 00:21:48.060
Living somewhere and they're writing code

00:21:48.060 --> 00:21:50.510
and they're incentivized
to compete with you, too.

00:21:51.420 --> 00:21:53.760
If you're not really following

00:21:53.760 --> 00:21:54.915
what are the classic four moats

00:21:54.915 --> 00:21:55.770
and building towards that,

00:21:55.770 --> 00:21:57.600
like, I think life gets a lot harder.

00:21:57.600 --> 00:21:58.433
Yes.

00:21:58.433 --> 00:22:00.870
Do you guys have a house view on

00:22:00.870 --> 00:22:05.010
the new competitive equilibrium

00:22:05.010 --> 00:22:08.700
with a lot of AI
engineering really working?

00:22:08.700 --> 00:22:09.690
Well, I think there are a couple things.

00:22:09.690 --> 00:22:12.450
I think Marc talked about
this a little while ago,

00:22:12.450 --> 00:22:14.880
but what is an EPD team?

00:22:14.880 --> 00:22:17.157
Engineering product design team.

00:22:17.157 --> 00:22:17.990
Product development team.

00:22:17.990 --> 00:22:19.530
You normally have one product manager,

00:22:19.530 --> 00:22:22.890
maybe you have five to eight
engineers and one designer.

00:22:22.890 --> 00:22:25.440
And now everybody has
Cursor and Claude Code.

00:22:25.440 --> 00:22:26.640
So the designer is like, "I don't need

00:22:26.640 --> 00:22:28.560
any of these bozos."

00:22:28.560 --> 00:22:30.840
The product manager says, "I
don't need any of those bozos."

00:22:30.840 --> 00:22:32.850
Each of the engineers are like,

00:22:32.850 --> 00:22:34.020
"I don't need any of these bozos."

00:22:34.020 --> 00:22:34.853
And they're all—

00:22:34.853 --> 00:22:35.807
It's like the start of "The Dark Knight."

00:22:35.807 --> 00:22:36.754
And they're all right.

00:22:36.754 --> 00:22:38.750
It really is, it's… Exactly.

00:22:38.750 --> 00:22:39.583
And they're all right.

00:22:39.583 --> 00:22:40.690
It's like they're all
wearing the Joker mask.

00:22:40.690 --> 00:22:42.420
Or, the Joker mask

00:22:42.420 --> 00:22:44.520
is Claude Code or Cursor.

00:22:44.520 --> 00:22:48.480
But the problem is, because I
was talking to one of my CEOs,

00:22:48.480 --> 00:22:51.510
this is a pretty scaled
company, lots of revenue.

00:22:51.510 --> 00:22:53.070
And they use all these tools,

00:22:53.070 --> 00:22:54.900
they're not more efficient.

00:22:54.900 --> 00:22:56.460
And he is like, "Why
aren't we more efficient?"

00:22:56.460 --> 00:22:57.293
And it's two things.

00:22:57.293 --> 00:22:58.680
Number one, it's a real company,

00:22:58.680 --> 00:22:59.550
they have real customers.

00:22:59.550 --> 00:23:02.100
They can't just push things
and hope for the best.

00:23:02.100 --> 00:23:03.690
But it's like you actually have

00:23:03.690 --> 00:23:05.580
an HR problem

00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:07.650
in that if you're building from scratch,

00:23:07.650 --> 00:23:09.540
granted you have nothing,
you have no tech debt,

00:23:09.540 --> 00:23:10.980
you don't really have to
worry about customers,

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:12.150
you don't have any,

00:23:12.150 --> 00:23:15.623
but you wouldn't have an
eight person EPD team.

00:23:15.623 --> 00:23:18.480
You would probably just
have each person...

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:20.160
We have eight different product managers

00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:21.540
slash engineers slash whatever.

00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:24.780
Each one is a pod unto themselves.

00:23:24.780 --> 00:23:26.013
So that's one thing.

00:23:27.090 --> 00:23:28.860
I totally agree on the data moats

00:23:28.860 --> 00:23:31.290
where that has become more important.

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:32.460
I'll tell you a story that you might like.

00:23:32.460 --> 00:23:34.410
I met this company, vLex.

00:23:34.410 --> 00:23:36.630
It's a 25-year-old company.

00:23:36.630 --> 00:23:40.860
This guy Alex bought up
every legal record in Spain,

00:23:40.860 --> 00:23:42.570
like probably going back to 1492.

00:23:42.570 --> 00:23:45.390
Buys the Ferdinand and Isabella...

00:23:45.390 --> 00:23:46.560
Here's the document,

00:23:46.560 --> 00:23:48.060
I'm going to take a picture of that,

00:23:48.060 --> 00:23:50.400
I'll sell it to every law firm.

00:23:50.400 --> 00:23:51.290
And he built like

00:23:51.290 --> 00:23:54.060
a $20 million a year SaaS business,

00:23:54.060 --> 00:23:55.950
bootstrapped for 25 years.

00:23:55.950 --> 00:23:58.140
And then it went to like a
hundred million in one year.

00:23:58.140 --> 00:23:59.490
Now why is that?

00:23:59.490 --> 00:24:01.530
Because he used to sell this document

00:24:01.530 --> 00:24:03.150
or he used to sell a subscription

00:24:03.150 --> 00:24:04.740
to Kirkland &amp; Ellis and Latham &amp; Watkins

00:24:04.740 --> 00:24:06.180
and all these big law firms.

00:24:06.180 --> 00:24:08.760
And a paralegal would take that,

00:24:08.760 --> 00:24:10.590
they would turn it into a document.

00:24:10.590 --> 00:24:13.080
They'd charge the client $10,000

00:24:13.080 --> 00:24:15.690
and ChatGPT can't do that.

00:24:15.690 --> 00:24:17.193
GenAI can't do that.

00:24:18.120 --> 00:24:20.370
But you know who can do that is vLex.

00:24:20.370 --> 00:24:23.760
Another good example of this,

00:24:23.760 --> 00:24:25.740
of who has proprietary data?

00:24:25.740 --> 00:24:27.600
A lot of times, the data's free.

00:24:27.600 --> 00:24:28.800
This is the really cool thing.

00:24:28.800 --> 00:24:31.320
Or it's sitting, I wouldn't
call your data free,

00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:34.440
but it's not like for-sale data sets.

00:24:34.440 --> 00:24:36.183
Do you know domaintools.com?

00:24:36.183 --> 00:24:37.016
It's my favorite business.

00:24:37.016 --> 00:24:38.700
So DomainTools

00:24:38.700 --> 00:24:43.080
runs a cron job every day on
every single internet website.

00:24:43.080 --> 00:24:44.997
They do a WHOIS lookup

00:24:46.260 --> 00:24:47.093
every single day.

00:24:47.093 --> 00:24:47.926
And they build a historical

00:24:47.926 --> 00:24:48.759
record of that which no one has.

00:24:48.759 --> 00:24:52.380
So if you want to see who
owned stripe.com in 1999,

00:24:52.380 --> 00:24:54.500
well, it was free in 1999.

00:24:54.500 --> 00:24:56.820
If you invented time machine,
go run that WHOIS query

00:24:56.820 --> 00:25:00.261
in your terminal but you can't do that.

00:25:00.261 --> 00:25:01.200
So you have to pay them.

00:25:01.200 --> 00:25:02.280
But all that data is free

00:25:02.280 --> 00:25:04.256
or FlightAware with ADS-B data.

00:25:04.256 --> 00:25:05.150
So you have a lot

00:25:05.150 --> 00:25:06.960
of these weird businesses.

00:25:06.960 --> 00:25:08.520
And of course this is not a new idea,

00:25:08.520 --> 00:25:09.750
like FactSet, Bloomberg...

00:25:09.750 --> 00:25:11.940
You have aggregators of data,

00:25:11.940 --> 00:25:13.800
but that becomes so much more valuable.

00:25:13.800 --> 00:25:14.633
Totally.

00:25:14.633 --> 00:25:17.640
Because your 10,000
geniuses cannot do that.

00:25:17.640 --> 00:25:18.473
100%.

00:25:18.473 --> 00:25:20.490
You always experience this if

00:25:20.490 --> 00:25:23.970
you hire someone extraordinarily talented,

00:25:23.970 --> 00:25:26.040
like top of the class at MIT

00:25:26.040 --> 00:25:29.550
and you say, "All right,
join the engineering team

00:25:29.550 --> 00:25:30.450
and go ship the code."

00:25:30.450 --> 00:25:32.400
And they ship something kind of crazy

00:25:32.400 --> 00:25:33.270
on the first day.

00:25:33.270 --> 00:25:34.103
And it's sort of slow.

00:25:34.103 --> 00:25:35.340
And it's like,

00:25:35.340 --> 00:25:38.250
you can have someone who is intellectually

00:25:38.250 --> 00:25:39.630
an absolute giant,

00:25:39.630 --> 00:25:43.470
but learning kind of how
a company does things

00:25:43.470 --> 00:25:44.850
the way it does.

00:25:44.850 --> 00:25:46.890
Learning the procedures and nuances

00:25:46.890 --> 00:25:48.390
of an organization takes time.

00:25:48.390 --> 00:25:50.160
It's part of what makes
working with interns

00:25:50.160 --> 00:25:52.020
or junior engineers fun.

00:25:52.020 --> 00:25:54.690
I think the learning curve
is obviously immensely steep.

00:25:54.690 --> 00:25:56.520
And I think that the speed at which,

00:25:56.520 --> 00:25:57.900
with sufficient access to the data

00:25:57.900 --> 00:25:59.850
and the procedures of a company,

00:25:59.850 --> 00:26:01.200
they're going to get
there very, very fast.

00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:04.440
But these
moats are real, you know?

00:26:04.440 --> 00:26:05.760
Well, the other moat

00:26:05.760 --> 00:26:07.800
I would argue is the dark-mattered moat.

00:26:07.800 --> 00:26:08.920
Yes.
And what I mean is

00:26:08.920 --> 00:26:11.040
you know, like the Milky Way,

00:26:11.040 --> 00:26:13.440
nobody could figure out
why the mass is the mass

00:26:13.440 --> 00:26:14.880
because it's like all the observed stars

00:26:14.880 --> 00:26:15.930
and planets and everything else.

00:26:15.930 --> 00:26:18.420
It's, no, actually the vast
majority of the matter,

00:26:18.420 --> 00:26:20.250
or energy, because they're the same thing,

00:26:20.250 --> 00:26:22.020
is dark matter, dark energy.

00:26:22.020 --> 00:26:23.520
And what does that mean?
Nobody the hell knows.

00:26:23.520 --> 00:26:25.200
But it's just like, we don't know it.

00:26:25.200 --> 00:26:26.940
And for products, it's like you built,

00:26:26.940 --> 00:26:31.170
if I tell Claude, "Go clone Ramp."

00:26:31.170 --> 00:26:33.390
Because this is the SaaSpocalypse

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:34.260
that's happening right now.

00:26:34.260 --> 00:26:35.190
It's like, oh, of course.

00:26:35.190 --> 00:26:36.270
Well, I just went to the website

00:26:36.270 --> 00:26:37.620
and now I have a website
that looks like Ramp.

00:26:37.620 --> 00:26:40.050
No, no, no. There are like
nine million edge cases.

00:26:40.050 --> 00:26:41.850
The tech debt sounds bad

00:26:41.850 --> 00:26:43.920
because it's a pejorative, debt = bad.

00:26:43.920 --> 00:26:45.180
But actually it's good

00:26:45.180 --> 00:26:47.070
because what you've
done is you've uncovered

00:26:47.070 --> 00:26:49.320
every single problem that can go wrong.

00:26:49.320 --> 00:26:51.510
The fact that, I mean, a great example,

00:26:51.510 --> 00:26:53.640
remember when the power
went out in San Francisco

00:26:53.640 --> 00:26:55.170
a month and a half ago?

00:26:55.170 --> 00:26:57.660
So we have this amazing
thing called Waymo.

00:26:57.660 --> 00:26:59.070
Waymo had not figured out

00:26:59.070 --> 00:27:00.360
this corner case

00:27:00.360 --> 00:27:02.070
of what if the power goes out?

00:27:02.070 --> 00:27:03.960
It was a thundering herd
problem with the customer,

00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:05.580
or the human overrides, yeah.

00:27:05.580 --> 00:27:07.080
But they had no idea.

00:27:07.080 --> 00:27:09.300
And it's actually great
that that happened.

00:27:09.300 --> 00:27:11.250
You need these problems to happen.

00:27:11.250 --> 00:27:12.083
For sure.

00:27:12.083 --> 00:27:13.470
And just because my background,

00:27:13.470 --> 00:27:14.373
I used to write shareware,

00:27:14.373 --> 00:27:16.170
this try-before-you-buy software.

00:27:16.170 --> 00:27:17.280
Shareware was a big thing.

00:27:17.280 --> 00:27:18.510
That's a blast from the past.

00:27:18.510 --> 00:27:19.470
It's a blast from the past.

00:27:19.470 --> 00:27:21.030
So there's a website that's part of CNET—

00:27:21.030 --> 00:27:22.237
That might come back, you know?

00:27:22.237 --> 00:27:24.270
Well, but it's freemium.

00:27:24.270 --> 00:27:25.260
This is where I'm going with this story

00:27:25.260 --> 00:27:26.093
and you'll like this.

00:27:26.093 --> 00:27:29.160
So Download.com was the preeminent site

00:27:29.160 --> 00:27:30.870
for downloading shareware.

00:27:30.870 --> 00:27:31.703
It was like...

00:27:31.703 --> 00:27:35.127
CNET was one of the most popular
properties in 1999, 2000.

00:27:35.127 --> 00:27:36.660
So, all of the top downloadable

00:27:36.660 --> 00:27:38.100
software products were there—

00:27:38.100 --> 00:27:39.360
Download.com sounds like a company

00:27:39.360 --> 00:27:41.100
that had a Super Bowl ad.

00:27:41.100 --> 00:27:41.933
They would've.

00:27:41.933 --> 00:27:43.080
I bet they did, actually.
They probably had two.

00:27:43.080 --> 00:27:45.270
I'd be shocked. They probably had five.

00:27:45.270 --> 00:27:47.250
Right between Pets.com and something else.

00:27:47.250 --> 00:27:50.730
So Download.com had all
the popular products

00:27:50.730 --> 00:27:53.190
and then this site called Elance shows up

00:27:53.190 --> 00:27:55.170
and Elance is now called Upwork.

00:27:55.170 --> 00:27:59.280
This connected you with work
that you would want done

00:27:59.280 --> 00:28:02.190
by very smart people, basically
in the developing world.

00:28:02.190 --> 00:28:03.600
So I want a software product built.

00:28:03.600 --> 00:28:06.360
Here's everybody in Romania
and Russia and India.

00:28:06.360 --> 00:28:08.910
I want translation, I want graphics.

00:28:08.910 --> 00:28:10.080
So what ended up happening

00:28:10.080 --> 00:28:12.180
is people would look at
the Download.com top list

00:28:12.180 --> 00:28:13.860
because before it was
a very cottage industry

00:28:13.860 --> 00:28:15.750
of who wrote shareware and
it was very profitable.

00:28:15.750 --> 00:28:18.660
Like ID software shareware company,

00:28:18.660 --> 00:28:20.640
McAfee shareware company.

00:28:20.640 --> 00:28:23.250
Actually Cybersource started
off processing payments

00:28:23.250 --> 00:28:24.420
for shareware companies.

00:28:24.420 --> 00:28:26.580
That's how they got McAfee.

00:28:26.580 --> 00:28:27.510
That's funny.

00:28:27.510 --> 00:28:28.680
Very interesting history to this

00:28:28.680 --> 00:28:29.790
we can talk about some other time,

00:28:29.790 --> 00:28:31.980
but basically what happened
is people found Elance,

00:28:31.980 --> 00:28:34.350
like, "Oh, I could pay anybody $500

00:28:34.350 --> 00:28:36.210
to clone anything on this list.

00:28:36.210 --> 00:28:37.320
Everything on this list makes

00:28:37.320 --> 00:28:39.300
tens of millions of dollars a year.

00:28:39.300 --> 00:28:40.380
Let's go."

00:28:40.380 --> 00:28:42.540
And the cloning never worked.

00:28:42.540 --> 00:28:43.860
It functionally worked.

00:28:43.860 --> 00:28:45.900
Because if I say "I'm
going to clone Eric."

00:28:45.900 --> 00:28:47.850
I might not know that you have a pancreas.

00:28:47.850 --> 00:28:48.683
Right?

00:28:48.683 --> 00:28:49.920
I'm sure you do.

00:28:49.920 --> 00:28:51.390
I might not know that
you have two kidneys.

00:28:51.390 --> 00:28:55.170
So the problem is that cloning
just, it goes skin deep

00:28:55.170 --> 00:28:59.190
and like that's the embedded
advantage of these companies.

00:28:59.190 --> 00:29:00.449
Do you then think

00:29:00.449 --> 00:29:02.340
the SaaSpocalypse is kind of irrational?

00:29:02.340 --> 00:29:03.180
I think it depends.

00:29:03.180 --> 00:29:04.500
I think some of it is very rational.

00:29:04.500 --> 00:29:06.630
Because if I want, if I have a feature

00:29:06.630 --> 00:29:07.800
that became a company,

00:29:07.800 --> 00:29:08.820
and I know this sounds

00:29:08.820 --> 00:29:10.830
like a very negative thing to say, but

00:29:10.830 --> 00:29:13.530
I want to get paged if
my server goes down.

00:29:13.530 --> 00:29:15.000
It's a very logical reason

00:29:15.000 --> 00:29:16.800
to build a company called PagerDuty.

00:29:18.090 --> 00:29:20.890
That now has a lot of stuff around it

00:29:21.810 --> 00:29:24.120
but I might just say, "Hey,
whenever my servers..."

00:29:24.120 --> 00:29:25.650
There are corner cases,

00:29:25.650 --> 00:29:28.080
there is the proverbial the
power goes out in San Francisco,

00:29:28.080 --> 00:29:29.070
do something differently.

00:29:29.070 --> 00:29:32.070
That's very, very different than NetSuite,

00:29:32.070 --> 00:29:33.540
which is, I have a saying that I like,

00:29:33.540 --> 00:29:35.610
which is the best companies
have hostages, not customers,

00:29:35.610 --> 00:29:36.630
at least in enterprise software.

00:29:36.630 --> 00:29:38.790
Not for you because
you actually have INPS.

00:29:38.790 --> 00:29:42.777
But that they're much
harder to go rip that out

00:29:42.777 --> 00:29:45.150
and a lot of it is like
the "Goldilocks zone"

00:29:45.150 --> 00:29:46.800
that you operate in.

00:29:46.800 --> 00:29:48.510
If you're too expensive,

00:29:48.510 --> 00:29:50.580
of course I'm going to
try to rip that thing out.

00:29:50.580 --> 00:29:54.150
If it's so cheap then I
forgot nobody even used it.

00:29:54.150 --> 00:29:56.220
So you have to be in this Goldilocks zone,

00:29:56.220 --> 00:29:57.870
you have to have enough complexity.

00:29:57.870 --> 00:30:00.930
And ideally the front end is
different than the back end.

00:30:00.930 --> 00:30:03.750
So, Workday I think is...

00:30:03.750 --> 00:30:06.383
Nobody's going to get rid of Workday.

00:30:06.383 --> 00:30:09.030
And I actually credit
David Ricardo with this.

00:30:09.030 --> 00:30:10.830
Like, comparative advantage.

00:30:10.830 --> 00:30:14.340
Sure, you could grow your own food.

00:30:14.340 --> 00:30:17.358
You could plumb your own plumbing.

00:30:17.358 --> 00:30:18.810
But you're not going to do that.

00:30:18.810 --> 00:30:20.160
And somebody could do your own HR system.

00:30:20.160 --> 00:30:21.817
Yes.

00:30:21.817 --> 00:30:25.920
The one maybe pitch for Stripe

00:30:25.920 --> 00:30:28.323
and others that I would say here is,

00:30:29.760 --> 00:30:32.340
I do think that that is right.

00:30:32.340 --> 00:30:34.140
And there is a lot of complexity

00:30:34.140 --> 00:30:37.590
and local tax law for payments

00:30:37.590 --> 00:30:39.840
and things that make Workday

00:30:39.840 --> 00:30:41.250
an extraordinary business.

00:30:41.250 --> 00:30:43.470
There was this overarching
macro question of

00:30:43.470 --> 00:30:45.001
how does work get done?

00:30:45.001 --> 00:30:46.680
If you believe that.

00:30:46.680 --> 00:30:50.310
You know, work can
get done through tokens

00:30:50.310 --> 00:30:51.483
and models.

00:30:52.530 --> 00:30:54.000
And presumably

00:30:54.000 --> 00:30:55.110
it doesn't go through payroll,

00:30:55.110 --> 00:30:58.200
probably goes through
a credit card or check

00:30:58.200 --> 00:30:59.310
to these types of companies.

00:30:59.310 --> 00:31:01.170
And so, the share of,

00:31:01.170 --> 00:31:03.090
it's almost this sort of simple math

00:31:03.090 --> 00:31:06.510
that if you X-rayed the
P&amp;L of most companies,

00:31:06.510 --> 00:31:07.830
the majority of it classically,

00:31:07.830 --> 00:31:10.770
at least for asset-light firms, is payroll.

00:31:10.770 --> 00:31:12.360
You're paying for people to do things

00:31:12.360 --> 00:31:15.988
and SaaS is maybe a small percentage

00:31:15.988 --> 00:31:20.897
of a company's income or
a company's cost base.

00:31:23.400 --> 00:31:28.400
Does suddenly that share
grow to high single digits,

00:31:28.530 --> 00:31:31.560
low double digits,
significant double digits?

00:31:31.560 --> 00:31:33.097
And does the share actually move where,

00:31:33.097 --> 00:31:35.490
the payroll economy itself,

00:31:35.490 --> 00:31:37.320
even though maybe it grows,
becomes less relevant?

00:31:37.320 --> 00:31:39.420
I think this is the
really funny part of even

00:31:39.420 --> 00:31:40.830
as this has been going on,

00:31:40.830 --> 00:31:42.570
what do I know as a private company

00:31:42.570 --> 00:31:44.490
other than seeing our own payment data.

00:31:44.490 --> 00:31:47.220
But you start to see these companies

00:31:47.220 --> 00:31:48.600
beating their earnings

00:31:48.600 --> 00:31:51.390
in a way they never have before where

00:31:51.390 --> 00:31:53.520
the terminal value perhaps is falling out.

00:31:53.520 --> 00:31:56.520
But they're saying,

00:31:56.520 --> 00:31:58.140
"Shareholders, we're reaccelerating."

00:31:58.140 --> 00:31:59.760
We haven't seen this since 2021.

00:31:59.760 --> 00:32:01.200
We're going faster and
you're going to have

00:32:01.200 --> 00:32:03.030
multiple progressive quarters of this

00:32:03.030 --> 00:32:04.950
actually occurring for
certain types of businesses.

00:32:04.950 --> 00:32:08.340
And so, it's a really
interesting time to...

00:32:08.340 --> 00:32:11.790
I very violently agree with your view

00:32:11.790 --> 00:32:13.823
is like yeah, it depends.

00:32:13.823 --> 00:32:15.450
But it's doing really well.

00:32:15.450 --> 00:32:16.860
That's my brilliant comment, it depends.

00:32:16.860 --> 00:32:18.243
Yeah, you mentioned...

00:32:19.740 --> 00:32:20.790
Very Howard Marks of you.

00:32:23.700 --> 00:32:24.870
As you're hearing from Eric,

00:32:24.870 --> 00:32:26.280
Ramp has become the default way

00:32:26.280 --> 00:32:28.440
a lot of American companies manage spend.

00:32:28.440 --> 00:32:30.150
Stablecoins allowed that
functionality to work

00:32:30.150 --> 00:32:32.580
in many countries all at once.

00:32:32.580 --> 00:32:34.920
With Ramp's stablecoin-backed
corporate cards,

00:32:34.920 --> 00:32:37.020
businesses can fund a
balance with stablecoins,

00:32:37.020 --> 00:32:39.180
issue cards against
those balances instantly,

00:32:39.180 --> 00:32:41.940
and allow employees to spend
anywhere cards are accepted

00:32:41.940 --> 00:32:43.350
without the business having to think about

00:32:43.350 --> 00:32:45.750
stablecoins all the time.

00:32:45.750 --> 00:32:48.210
Same card experience, same controls,

00:32:48.210 --> 00:32:50.760
just a much more global
set of rails underneath.

00:32:50.760 --> 00:32:52.560
This is one of the many practical ways

00:32:52.560 --> 00:32:54.450
we're seeing businesses
on Stripe use stablecoins

00:32:54.450 --> 00:32:57.240
by launching card programs
in many more countries

00:32:57.240 --> 00:32:58.770
and doing so in much less time

00:32:58.770 --> 00:33:00.120
than it would've taken otherwise.

00:33:00.120 --> 00:33:02.490
If you're thinking about
using stablecoins to expand,

00:33:02.490 --> 00:33:03.323
Stripe can help.

00:33:06.630 --> 00:33:08.820
You mentioned your spend data.

00:33:08.820 --> 00:33:11.100
What does the spend data that Ramp sees

00:33:11.100 --> 00:33:12.400
tell us about the economy?

00:33:14.460 --> 00:33:15.660
I think it is stronger

00:33:17.940 --> 00:33:22.940
than many people
understand in lots of ways.

00:33:23.070 --> 00:33:25.290
I mean first, I'll go
back to one of the things

00:33:25.290 --> 00:33:28.173
that perplexed our team for a long time.

00:33:28.173 --> 00:33:31.530
Our economists on the team

00:33:31.530 --> 00:33:32.490
saw this data

00:33:32.490 --> 00:33:34.320
even as recently as last year

00:33:34.320 --> 00:33:38.520
were the Census Bureau would
do these periodic surveys.

00:33:38.520 --> 00:33:41.520
They'd go out and ask

00:33:41.520 --> 00:33:44.100
"How much is your business using AI

00:33:44.100 --> 00:33:45.540
to produce goods or services?"

00:33:45.540 --> 00:33:48.390
A very refined economic way

00:33:48.390 --> 00:33:49.440
of wording the questions.

00:33:49.440 --> 00:33:51.390
And they would come back

00:33:51.390 --> 00:33:52.890
with these pronouncements saying

00:33:52.890 --> 00:33:55.080
a single digit percent
of businesses in the US

00:33:55.080 --> 00:33:56.711
have adopted AI.

00:33:56.711 --> 00:33:59.850
And we looked at our data and

00:33:59.850 --> 00:34:02.340
we support over 55,000 businesses.

00:34:02.340 --> 00:34:05.580
We lean a little bit towards
tech but not heavily.

00:34:05.580 --> 00:34:07.530
It kind of resembles the
distribution of businesses

00:34:07.530 --> 00:34:09.450
you would see in the States.

00:34:09.450 --> 00:34:14.247
And well, the majority of
businesses have used AI.

00:34:14.247 --> 00:34:16.620
You look at businesses
whether they're paying for—

00:34:16.620 --> 00:34:18.510
As in they subscribe
to ChatGPT or Anthropic

00:34:18.510 --> 00:34:19.680
or something like that.
Exactly.

00:34:19.680 --> 00:34:22.263
Or, maybe a business that is a true

00:34:23.453 --> 00:34:24.286
agentic… Cognition

00:34:24.286 --> 00:34:25.119
or a Cursor, something like that

00:34:26.357 --> 00:34:29.220
where this is kind of
vertical application.

00:34:29.220 --> 00:34:32.460
And so, one, there is
this disconnect between

00:34:32.460 --> 00:34:34.953
the use of tools if you look at

00:34:34.953 --> 00:34:36.960
how quickly businesses are adopting

00:34:36.960 --> 00:34:39.510
and responding to these new tools

00:34:39.510 --> 00:34:43.500
versus what maybe people
report on. Next: growth.

00:34:43.500 --> 00:34:46.440
I think it's been clear
over the last few quarters

00:34:46.440 --> 00:34:48.867
is the US itself is reaccelerating,

00:34:48.867 --> 00:34:52.800
GDP growth is gone from
maybe the one to 2%,

00:34:52.800 --> 00:34:54.990
area to four to five

00:34:54.990 --> 00:34:55.980
and you could argue how much of this is,

00:34:55.980 --> 00:34:57.540
a little artificial with subsidies,

00:34:57.540 --> 00:34:59.370
but it's been pretty significant.

00:34:59.370 --> 00:35:00.510
But subsidized by what?

00:35:00.510 --> 00:35:01.343
Some of the Big Beautiful Bill

00:35:02.426 --> 00:35:05.167
as well as some of the tariffs.

00:35:05.167 --> 00:35:06.450
And where that's been reallocated,

00:35:06.450 --> 00:35:08.490
I think some people, I would argue

00:35:08.490 --> 00:35:09.910
on the whole maybe unfairly

00:35:09.910 --> 00:35:11.280
said this.

00:35:11.280 --> 00:35:14.100
I think that there's more durable growth

00:35:14.100 --> 00:35:15.570
inside of the businesses.

00:35:15.570 --> 00:35:16.990
But I would say

00:35:18.210 --> 00:35:21.090
overall business health is much stronger.

00:35:21.090 --> 00:35:22.770
And I think that the
really interesting thing

00:35:22.770 --> 00:35:25.020
and again some of this is
specific to the data that we see,

00:35:25.020 --> 00:35:27.780
but I think that businesses are generally

00:35:27.780 --> 00:35:30.840
getting more and more
savvy about finding tools

00:35:30.840 --> 00:35:32.280
that help them be more efficient.

00:35:32.280 --> 00:35:34.740
And I think what skews our data

00:35:34.740 --> 00:35:39.270
is maybe the savings we drive for people.

00:35:39.270 --> 00:35:42.630
Seems the way of explaining
it is like people know the,

00:35:42.630 --> 00:35:44.010
Ben Franklin would say this phrase

00:35:44.010 --> 00:35:45.390
of "a penny saved is a penny earned"

00:35:45.390 --> 00:35:47.520
and this is awesome aphorism

00:35:47.520 --> 00:35:49.470
but the average American business

00:35:49.470 --> 00:35:51.480
has an 8% profit margin.

00:35:51.480 --> 00:35:55.140
And so mathematically speaking,
a penny saved is equivalent

00:35:55.140 --> 00:35:57.960
to 12 pennies of revenue earned.

00:35:57.960 --> 00:35:59.970
And so, if you save businesses a lot,

00:35:59.970 --> 00:36:01.230
you end up with these outcomes

00:36:01.230 --> 00:36:04.710
of the average Ramp bases
materially outgrowing

00:36:04.710 --> 00:36:06.360
the regular US average.

00:36:06.360 --> 00:36:09.210
And we can clearly see this

00:36:09.210 --> 00:36:11.010
and demonstrate this for
our own set of customers.

00:36:11.010 --> 00:36:13.920
I suspect these types of things

00:36:13.920 --> 00:36:15.870
are occurring at businesses adopting

00:36:15.870 --> 00:36:17.310
these new sets of technologies,

00:36:17.310 --> 00:36:19.710
I would argue probably faster,

00:36:19.710 --> 00:36:20.880
very clearly, than what the US

00:36:20.880 --> 00:36:23.610
is maybe, the classic sources are seeing.

00:36:23.610 --> 00:36:25.260
So I think it's much healthier.

00:36:25.260 --> 00:36:27.900
When a business saves money
on Ramp, what happens?

00:36:27.900 --> 00:36:31.470
The CFO comes in

00:36:31.470 --> 00:36:33.397
and says, "Oh my god, we're spending—

00:36:33.397 --> 00:36:35.160
Confetti.
"Way too much money,

00:36:35.160 --> 00:36:36.120
on confetti.

00:36:36.120 --> 00:36:37.260
We don't need this much confetti."

00:36:37.260 --> 00:36:39.527
Who's buying the confetti?

00:36:39.527 --> 00:36:41.370
"We have huge stockpiles
that we don't need."

00:36:41.370 --> 00:36:43.620
What happens when a business saves 5%?

00:36:43.620 --> 00:36:45.623
What are they actually cutting?

00:36:45.623 --> 00:36:48.000
So there's two pieces of it primarily.

00:36:48.000 --> 00:36:50.430
One is like these hard-dollar cost savings

00:36:50.430 --> 00:36:51.327
and then some of this is time,

00:36:51.327 --> 00:36:53.550
and I'll start with the time
because it's the squishy one,

00:36:53.550 --> 00:36:55.410
but I think that it's, in some cases,

00:36:55.410 --> 00:36:56.760
actually much more important.

00:36:56.760 --> 00:36:59.430
Oh sure, they save time on
their expense reconciliation.

00:36:59.430 --> 00:37:01.096
That makes sense.

00:37:01.096 --> 00:37:02.490
So they're no longer
going through line by line

00:37:02.490 --> 00:37:03.930
and therefore there's

00:37:03.930 --> 00:37:06.780
one person who is freed up
to go do something else.

00:37:06.780 --> 00:37:07.613
Yeah.

00:37:07.613 --> 00:37:10.590
And I think one of the classic biases

00:37:10.590 --> 00:37:12.600
that people forget is people chronically

00:37:12.600 --> 00:37:14.640
undervalue their own time.

00:37:14.640 --> 00:37:17.944
As a principle of business,

00:37:17.944 --> 00:37:21.405
everyone has an hourly rate.

00:37:21.405 --> 00:37:23.190
Within a business, human
time is incredibly expensive.

00:37:23.190 --> 00:37:25.740
I think the lean companies got this right

00:37:25.740 --> 00:37:27.270
and eliminated the bottlenecks there.

00:37:27.270 --> 00:37:28.389
So, okay, that's one form.

00:37:28.389 --> 00:37:29.550
What else?

00:37:29.550 --> 00:37:31.260
Next, all right, when you start to have

00:37:31.260 --> 00:37:33.180
these fine-tuned kind of controls.

00:37:33.180 --> 00:37:36.270
This part has been I think probably

00:37:36.270 --> 00:37:39.030
the biggest lion's share of this.

00:37:39.030 --> 00:37:41.310
Let's go back to one of
the earlier conversations

00:37:41.310 --> 00:37:42.990
of why do people use checks,

00:37:42.990 --> 00:37:45.030
this horrible system and purchase orders.

00:37:45.030 --> 00:37:46.950
Well, one of the advantages of checks

00:37:46.950 --> 00:37:50.130
is unless you mail it to
someone and you sign it,

00:37:50.130 --> 00:37:52.410
no money can leave your company.

00:37:52.410 --> 00:37:54.810
And what every person knows

00:37:54.810 --> 00:37:57.210
who's ever had a credit
card and gone to a gym once

00:37:57.210 --> 00:37:58.920
and had a good New Year's resolution.

00:37:58.920 --> 00:38:00.990
But is you go a couple times

00:38:00.990 --> 00:38:02.910
and you're like, "Goddammit,
this gym is charging me."

00:38:02.910 --> 00:38:04.800
The difficulty of sending a
check is a feature, not a bug.

00:38:04.800 --> 00:38:05.790
Yeah, exactly.

00:38:05.790 --> 00:38:07.120
But it's part of what Ramp

00:38:08.010 --> 00:38:10.200
was the first to build.
One-time use cards.

00:38:10.200 --> 00:38:12.630
Single-use cards, but also more than that,

00:38:12.630 --> 00:38:13.713
merchant blocking.

00:38:14.640 --> 00:38:16.620
So you can, whether it's on one card

00:38:16.620 --> 00:38:18.540
or 10,000 cards at once,

00:38:18.540 --> 00:38:20.733
build in a kill switch to say, "Okay,

00:38:21.570 --> 00:38:24.270
we've signed this new
deal with this merchant.

00:38:24.270 --> 00:38:26.070
We are only on Uber,

00:38:26.070 --> 00:38:27.690
we are no longer taking Lyfts.

00:38:27.690 --> 00:38:30.810
And anytime someone goes and
tries to go on merchant A or B,

00:38:30.810 --> 00:38:31.643
you can do this."

00:38:31.643 --> 00:38:33.247
Or you sign a new contract and you say,

00:38:33.247 --> 00:38:37.260
"I'm going spend $10,000
only with Salesforce

00:38:37.260 --> 00:38:40.381
and on the 10,001th dollar
they try to charge you,

00:38:40.381 --> 00:38:41.670
it declines."

00:38:41.670 --> 00:38:43.860
And what gets to happen, you
get to have a conversation

00:38:43.860 --> 00:38:46.590
with their sales team who really
wants to make their budget.

00:38:46.590 --> 00:38:48.810
And if you look at this mathematically,

00:38:48.810 --> 00:38:51.540
for most companies that
come over a low end,

00:38:51.540 --> 00:38:53.430
maybe a very hygienic company

00:38:53.430 --> 00:38:55.560
that is giving out a few cards

00:38:55.560 --> 00:38:57.090
and the old world

00:38:57.090 --> 00:38:58.290
could durably cut their expenses

00:38:58.290 --> 00:38:59.910
about 2% a year by doing this.

00:38:59.910 --> 00:39:02.310
Some of these very
laissez-faire businesses

00:39:02.310 --> 00:39:06.030
are saving just 10%, just
through these things.

00:39:06.030 --> 00:39:08.670
Next, you start ending up
with these more fine-tuned

00:39:08.670 --> 00:39:09.990
kind of controls where you can say

00:39:09.990 --> 00:39:11.280
under these circumstances

00:39:11.280 --> 00:39:14.160
I want to go and have
kind of charges occur.

00:39:14.160 --> 00:39:17.460
Maybe you have an engineer
stay till 8:00 PM at night,

00:39:17.460 --> 00:39:20.250
you can go and buy a meal.

00:39:20.250 --> 00:39:22.860
But if you take an Uber on a
Saturday, that should turn off.

00:39:22.860 --> 00:39:23.820
And so you have the cards,

00:39:23.820 --> 00:39:27.360
if it's like an Uber on
a Saturday auto-decline,

00:39:27.360 --> 00:39:29.520
but you can text and say
actually it's for work.

00:39:29.520 --> 00:39:31.440
You send back a yes, it turns on.

00:39:31.440 --> 00:39:33.360
And so it's all these
little tiny paper cuts

00:39:33.360 --> 00:39:34.860
that actually start to go into run.

00:39:34.860 --> 00:39:36.360
Next, it's vendor data.

00:39:36.360 --> 00:39:39.540
One of the unique assets of Ramp

00:39:39.540 --> 00:39:42.000
is anytime you spend money,
you upload a receipt,

00:39:42.000 --> 00:39:44.100
maybe you upload an invoice or an MSA.

00:39:44.100 --> 00:39:46.920
This takes us back to the Paribus days.

00:39:46.920 --> 00:39:51.000
We know across hundreds of
millions of purchases every year,

00:39:51.000 --> 00:39:54.330
not just that you spent
money at a software vendor,

00:39:54.330 --> 00:39:57.060
but what did you spend per seat?

00:39:57.060 --> 00:39:58.860
And so if you're a customer on Ramp

00:39:58.860 --> 00:40:01.170
and you're about to go
and pay this large bill

00:40:01.170 --> 00:40:03.210
on a random SaaS vendor,

00:40:03.210 --> 00:40:04.560
we can show you in real-time

00:40:04.560 --> 00:40:06.480
before you send the funds,

00:40:06.480 --> 00:40:09.603
you are paying 20% more
than the rest of the market.

00:40:10.470 --> 00:40:12.270
Here's kind of the cost curve

00:40:12.270 --> 00:40:13.620
of what others are paying.

00:40:13.620 --> 00:40:16.340
And actually by going and
empowering your procurement team—

00:40:16.340 --> 00:40:18.000
Do you have enough data to do that?

00:40:18.000 --> 00:40:20.993
Because my AWS bill
is not like your AWS bill.

00:40:20.993 --> 00:40:22.350
And so, don't you need to know

00:40:22.350 --> 00:40:23.670
how many instances we're running

00:40:23.670 --> 00:40:25.860
to know if this is a good deal or not?

00:40:25.860 --> 00:40:30.180
You can, but you can still
kind of get down to the level

00:40:30.180 --> 00:40:31.980
of is there a bulk discount or not?

00:40:31.980 --> 00:40:34.800
And start to know of, is
there a negotiated aspect?

00:40:34.800 --> 00:40:38.230
There's other types of
things where I'd argue

00:40:39.630 --> 00:40:41.100
maybe a lot of classic software-

00:40:41.100 --> 00:40:42.300
as-a-service ends up in this way.

00:40:42.300 --> 00:40:44.940
It's like your pricing is
really dependent on, you know,

00:40:44.940 --> 00:40:47.070
did you sign that deal on January 1st

00:40:47.070 --> 00:40:49.080
or on December 31st when the sales team

00:40:49.080 --> 00:40:50.340
really needs to hit the quota.

00:40:50.340 --> 00:40:53.640
And you can actually see
down to the seat level,

00:40:53.640 --> 00:40:55.420
you're paying $30 per seat.

00:40:55.420 --> 00:40:57.930
It's a great time to be
signing a Salesforce contract.

00:40:57.930 --> 00:40:59.753
Exactly.

00:40:59.753 --> 00:41:01.650
And so you can actually get
down to the seat level and say,

00:41:01.650 --> 00:41:04.200
okay, you're about to auto-renew.

00:41:04.200 --> 00:41:07.650
The market price has actually
moved for the set of service.

00:41:07.650 --> 00:41:10.170
And so it can be very, very
useful to procurement teams

00:41:10.170 --> 00:41:13.770
to know where they
compare with the market.

00:41:13.770 --> 00:41:15.270
That was actually my question for you,

00:41:15.270 --> 00:41:17.940
which is, so if you remember Groupon—

00:41:17.940 --> 00:41:19.230
Yes.
The much beleaguered Groupon,

00:41:19.230 --> 00:41:22.209
but Groupon started off as a site called

00:41:22.209 --> 00:41:24.390
ThePoint.com/groupon

00:41:24.390 --> 00:41:25.470
because originally it was like,

00:41:25.470 --> 00:41:28.260
"I don't like the fact that
Starbucks uses paper cups.

00:41:28.260 --> 00:41:30.960
If I get 10,000 other
people to donate a dollar,

00:41:30.960 --> 00:41:33.690
we'll fly a plane in front
of Howard Schultz's house,"

00:41:33.690 --> 00:41:35.490
which also emits carbon, a bad example.

00:41:35.490 --> 00:41:39.053
But you only want, it's a
collective action thing.

00:41:39.053 --> 00:41:41.040
So, and then because I met

00:41:41.040 --> 00:41:43.050
Andrew Mason and Brad Keywell

00:41:43.050 --> 00:41:44.490
when they were first
starting this business,

00:41:44.490 --> 00:41:45.510
it was like eight people.

00:41:45.510 --> 00:41:46.500
It was ThePoint

00:41:46.500 --> 00:41:48.510
and downstairs there was a
pizza restaurant or something

00:41:48.510 --> 00:41:50.760
like, "Oh, if get like 50 people

00:41:50.760 --> 00:41:54.480
to agree to buy pizza, maybe
they'll give us a lower price."

00:41:54.480 --> 00:41:55.620
The cause became discounts.

00:41:55.620 --> 00:41:57.330
Yes and then, but then the thing is

00:41:57.330 --> 00:42:00.030
Groupon wasn't known
for this "it has to tip"

00:42:00.030 --> 00:42:02.490
because they always had the demand.

00:42:02.490 --> 00:42:03.810
So they never had to worry about it

00:42:03.810 --> 00:42:05.220
because before it's
like you've got supply,

00:42:05.220 --> 00:42:06.450
you've got demand.

00:42:06.450 --> 00:42:08.340
I guess my question for you is,

00:42:08.340 --> 00:42:11.010
can you be the arbiter of pricing?

00:42:11.010 --> 00:42:12.570
Can you aggregate the demand?

00:42:12.570 --> 00:42:14.880
And the same way that like
Costco, who shops at Costco?

00:42:14.880 --> 00:42:17.363
Every small restaurant shops at Costco.

00:42:17.363 --> 00:42:19.890
And Costco uses that
collective bargaining power,

00:42:19.890 --> 00:42:21.900
if you will, to say, "Hey Coca-Cola,

00:42:21.900 --> 00:42:23.910
give us a very, very low price."

00:42:23.910 --> 00:42:25.247
They take very, very little of that

00:42:25.247 --> 00:42:28.080
because right now I'm
using Ramp to buy stuff.

00:42:28.080 --> 00:42:28.913
Yes.

00:42:28.913 --> 00:42:30.997
But I would imagine if you could say,

00:42:30.997 --> 00:42:32.662
"Hey, I have $100 billion."

00:42:32.662 --> 00:42:34.179
I didn't know it was that
high, that's amazing.

00:42:34.179 --> 00:42:35.760
"I have a hundred
billion dollars of spend.

00:42:35.760 --> 00:42:38.550
I can hopefully direct
it this way or that way.

00:42:38.550 --> 00:42:40.800
Please give me a 20% discount."

00:42:40.800 --> 00:42:41.770
Can you do that?

00:42:41.770 --> 00:42:44.400
Or is that further in the funnel

00:42:44.400 --> 00:42:45.840
of the purchase process?

00:42:45.840 --> 00:42:49.860
There is this large swath of businesses

00:42:49.860 --> 00:42:51.810
that I've been fascinated by

00:42:51.810 --> 00:42:53.700
called group purchasing organizations.

00:42:53.700 --> 00:42:55.020
A lot of these

00:42:55.020 --> 00:42:57.180
came out of the healthcare world

00:42:57.180 --> 00:42:59.730
where it's a very small set

00:42:59.730 --> 00:43:00.563
of—
APMs, yeah.

00:43:00.563 --> 00:43:01.440
Yeah, exactly, supplier.

00:43:01.440 --> 00:43:03.360
But if you can go and aggregate demand,

00:43:03.360 --> 00:43:05.550
you could say, okay, for
these type of purchases,

00:43:05.550 --> 00:43:06.990
you're going to offer this procedure

00:43:06.990 --> 00:43:08.400
at this account for
this type of equipment,

00:43:08.400 --> 00:43:10.170
you're going to give this
level bulk kind of discount.

00:43:10.170 --> 00:43:11.400
And you've gone and you've done that

00:43:11.400 --> 00:43:13.260
and I think these are very popular now

00:43:13.260 --> 00:43:14.504
in the private equity world.

00:43:14.504 --> 00:43:16.650
Even when I look at Ramp data today,

00:43:16.650 --> 00:43:18.627
there are dozens of,

00:43:18.627 --> 00:43:20.820
and more and more every
year, of merchants where,

00:43:20.820 --> 00:43:24.900
you know, we were sending
billions of dollars.

00:43:24.900 --> 00:43:26.760
It is truly, it is the client's money,

00:43:26.760 --> 00:43:28.080
they are going where they're going.

00:43:28.080 --> 00:43:30.240
But we have a sense of actually,

00:43:30.240 --> 00:43:31.830
okay, where is this actually going?

00:43:31.830 --> 00:43:36.450
And can you go and say, you
know, across the Ramp buyer base

00:43:36.450 --> 00:43:38.040
over the next 12 months,

00:43:38.040 --> 00:43:40.200
this is how many dollars
will go towards you.

00:43:40.200 --> 00:43:41.520
Can we negotiate a discount?

00:43:41.520 --> 00:43:45.000
The other way which maybe starts to...

00:43:45.000 --> 00:43:47.820
Sometimes these businesses
veer closer to advertising,

00:43:47.820 --> 00:43:49.530
but where it gets really interesting

00:43:49.530 --> 00:43:53.070
is we see the fastest-growing businesses.

00:43:53.070 --> 00:43:54.120
What are the businesses

00:43:54.120 --> 00:43:55.740
that people are getting really excited

00:43:55.740 --> 00:43:58.530
and are adopting really quickly?

00:43:58.530 --> 00:43:59.520
And we might have a signal of

00:43:59.520 --> 00:44:01.260
these are actually good
businesses and good tools

00:44:01.260 --> 00:44:04.620
and actually most businesses
should move towards that.

00:44:04.620 --> 00:44:06.997
Could you go and actually start to say,

00:44:06.997 --> 00:44:09.963
"Hey, your renewal is
coming up in 90 days.

00:44:11.520 --> 00:44:13.650
Have you considered this other business

00:44:13.650 --> 00:44:17.310
who has provided a 20%
complimentary welcome

00:44:17.310 --> 00:44:18.270
lower price to you?"

00:44:18.270 --> 00:44:20.130
And so, there's multiple
forms that can take place.

00:44:20.130 --> 00:44:22.530
But the short version of it is, I think

00:44:22.530 --> 00:44:26.040
one, it's yes, I think you
can offer a bulk discount.

00:44:26.040 --> 00:44:30.060
But two, you end up
almost getting closer to,

00:44:30.060 --> 00:44:33.180
back to this other theme
of can you start to go

00:44:33.180 --> 00:44:36.750
and maybe show businesses

00:44:36.750 --> 00:44:39.540
that directing their next marginal dollar

00:44:39.540 --> 00:44:41.910
will lead to this outcome.

00:44:41.910 --> 00:44:44.073
Well, it feels like there's
even like a third thing.

00:44:44.073 --> 00:44:45.060
Which is,

00:44:45.060 --> 00:44:48.030
I would call it merchant-specific balance.

00:44:48.030 --> 00:44:50.940
So imagine that it's December 31st,

00:44:50.940 --> 00:44:52.950
I'm buying something at Whole Foods.

00:44:52.950 --> 00:44:54.870
And then I see an offer,
not donate a dollar

00:44:54.870 --> 00:44:57.360
to the, you know, whatever
charity they're pushing.

00:44:57.360 --> 00:44:59.587
But it's like, why don't you commit

00:44:59.587 --> 00:45:04.587
$2,000 of spend for 2026 for $1,800?

00:45:05.873 --> 00:45:08.010
And the nice thing there
is that they lock in

00:45:08.010 --> 00:45:09.180
that spend for me.

00:45:09.180 --> 00:45:10.110
They actually collect it.

00:45:10.110 --> 00:45:11.940
I mean I know there's a stupid law

00:45:11.940 --> 00:45:12.900
and all this complicated stuff.

00:45:12.900 --> 00:45:15.390
But the key thing is it's
almost a risk management

00:45:15.390 --> 00:45:17.853
and a loyalty play for them at once.

00:45:19.020 --> 00:45:20.790
Because the problem is
that they don't know

00:45:20.790 --> 00:45:22.470
how much celery to buy.

00:45:22.470 --> 00:45:23.310
They don't know how many...

00:45:23.310 --> 00:45:25.440
So, it kind goes back to the AR/AP thing

00:45:25.440 --> 00:45:26.730
that we were talking about earlier.

00:45:26.730 --> 00:45:27.563
You nailed it, yeah.

00:45:27.563 --> 00:45:28.927
Because the other thing
is not just saying,

00:45:28.927 --> 00:45:31.410
"Hey, I'm going to group bargain

00:45:31.410 --> 00:45:34.410
on behalf of all the different
customers that I work with."

00:45:34.410 --> 00:45:37.590
But it's an ancillary
thing, which is you might

00:45:37.590 --> 00:45:40.770
want to just pre-commit your
spend to a particular vendor.

00:45:40.770 --> 00:45:43.680
And that vendor would love to
have that spend pre-committed,

00:45:43.680 --> 00:45:44.580
even if they're Amazon

00:45:44.580 --> 00:45:46.973
and their cost of capital is very low.

00:45:46.973 --> 00:45:48.630
Because they know they don't have to worry

00:45:48.630 --> 00:45:50.610
when they're planning their
budget for the next year.

00:45:50.610 --> 00:45:51.443
100%.

00:45:51.443 --> 00:45:52.590
That you're going to churn in month nine.

00:45:52.590 --> 00:45:54.210
And that seems like another,

00:45:54.210 --> 00:45:57.953
like the closest that you can
get to that is gift cards.

00:45:57.953 --> 00:45:58.920
Strangely enough which
I'm sure you know well

00:45:58.920 --> 00:45:59.753
from your—

00:45:59.753 --> 00:46:00.586
It's gift cards

00:46:00.586 --> 00:46:01.419
for business.
Yeah because it's like

00:46:01.419 --> 00:46:03.150
you go to Costco, this is
actually a big business at Costco.

00:46:03.150 --> 00:46:05.160
You go to Costco, they sell gift cards.

00:46:05.160 --> 00:46:06.030
We were just talking about this.

00:46:06.030 --> 00:46:07.050
So they sell gift cards.

00:46:07.050 --> 00:46:10.530
You can buy $100 of Starbucks
gift cards for 80 bucks.

00:46:10.530 --> 00:46:11.610
Is Starbucks dumb?

00:46:11.610 --> 00:46:13.440
Like why are they, if they're worth $100?

00:46:13.440 --> 00:46:16.560
No, because they basically
have, in the ecosystem,

00:46:16.560 --> 00:46:19.110
money that is going to
be spent at Starbucks.

00:46:19.110 --> 00:46:20.610
And yeah, there's
breakage and other stuff,

00:46:20.610 --> 00:46:21.960
but it's actually a quite,

00:46:21.960 --> 00:46:24.090
it's quite a valuable value proposition

00:46:24.090 --> 00:46:26.040
for the receiving merchant

00:46:26.040 --> 00:46:27.510
because they know that
you're going to spend

00:46:27.510 --> 00:46:28.500
your money with them.

00:46:28.500 --> 00:46:30.630
But I want to go back
to your previous idea

00:46:30.630 --> 00:46:33.420
where McDonald's every
five years or whatever

00:46:33.420 --> 00:46:35.130
does a big bake-off between Coke and Pepsi

00:46:35.130 --> 00:46:36.000
and they always renew with Coke

00:46:36.000 --> 00:46:36.833
and they have a long-time…

00:46:36.833 --> 00:46:39.743
Maybe a movie theater
chain is better example.

00:46:39.743 --> 00:46:41.940
All the big chains, like they
don't serve Coke and Pepsi,

00:46:41.940 --> 00:46:44.940
they only serve one and
they do a big bake-off

00:46:44.940 --> 00:46:47.490
between the brands every five years.

00:46:47.490 --> 00:46:49.530
And you have lots of small businesses

00:46:49.530 --> 00:46:51.570
that are buying from Coke
or buying from Pepsi.

00:46:51.570 --> 00:46:53.430
And so, it feels like you
could just do a bake-off

00:46:53.430 --> 00:46:56.250
between them saying Ramp is
going to have a preferred vendor

00:46:56.250 --> 00:47:00.150
and you can obviously use a
Ramp card on either, it'll work,

00:47:00.150 --> 00:47:03.630
but we have preferred rates with Pepsi.

00:47:03.630 --> 00:47:05.460
Is that a thing Ramp should do?

00:47:05.460 --> 00:47:07.170
It's a really interesting question.

00:47:07.170 --> 00:47:10.620
The short is I think over
the long duration of time.

00:47:10.620 --> 00:47:13.410
Like I'm kind of bit of a crazy person.

00:47:13.410 --> 00:47:16.570
We should try everything
is my actual answer.

00:47:16.570 --> 00:47:17.790
Claude Code.
Yeah, Claude Code.

00:47:17.790 --> 00:47:18.623
Let's go, let's do it,

00:47:18.623 --> 00:47:19.456
build it.

00:47:19.456 --> 00:47:20.640
Pepsi. Make money from Pepsi.
Go and negotiate.

00:47:20.640 --> 00:47:21.780
The cost of a phone call,

00:47:21.780 --> 00:47:24.168
a negotiation has never been lower, but…

00:47:24.168 --> 00:47:27.000
And there's this almost
philosophical question of like,

00:47:27.000 --> 00:47:30.210
all right, where is the differentiation?

00:47:30.210 --> 00:47:32.970
What makes Ramp different?

00:47:32.970 --> 00:47:34.930
But isn't that increasingly the scale?

00:47:34.930 --> 00:47:37.990
It is, and I think that
you're right in that

00:47:39.135 --> 00:47:41.190
at the very beginning of the company,

00:47:41.190 --> 00:47:43.380
the answer was very easy of like, look,

00:47:43.380 --> 00:47:46.923
our cost of capital is
nowhere close to a bank's.

00:47:48.180 --> 00:47:51.090
The interchange that we could
make is nowhere close to it.

00:47:51.090 --> 00:47:54.240
We have all the disadvantages of scale

00:47:54.240 --> 00:47:56.280
and kind of the only
thing that we could do

00:47:56.280 --> 00:47:58.650
to compete with these people was we could…

00:47:58.650 --> 00:48:00.457
Everything was slow at

00:48:00.457 --> 00:48:02.790
the bank that I worked at before,

00:48:02.790 --> 00:48:04.590
it was hard to ship things.

00:48:04.590 --> 00:48:06.090
We need to move fast.

00:48:06.090 --> 00:48:08.220
We need to kind of have this
emphasis around velocity

00:48:08.220 --> 00:48:10.620
to ship product and just
kind of blitzkrieg faster

00:48:10.620 --> 00:48:12.630
than maybe others could respond.

00:48:12.630 --> 00:48:14.640
What it's evolved into

00:48:14.640 --> 00:48:16.180
and I think is today probably

00:48:17.205 --> 00:48:19.950
the largest point of
differentiation at the meta level

00:48:19.950 --> 00:48:24.950
between Ramp and maybe the
financial service providers

00:48:25.020 --> 00:48:28.830
that we compete with is they sell money

00:48:28.830 --> 00:48:29.943
and we sell time.

00:48:31.110 --> 00:48:32.100
They'll sell you a lower,

00:48:32.100 --> 00:48:35.580
like a loan at a lower cost of capital,

00:48:35.580 --> 00:48:37.830
rewards at wherever they set it.

00:48:37.830 --> 00:48:40.560
We will sell your expenses, done.

00:48:40.560 --> 00:48:42.240
And so, are you naturally
skeptical of this idea

00:48:42.240 --> 00:48:44.670
because it's selling money
rather than selling time?

00:48:44.670 --> 00:48:46.230
I wouldn't say I'm naturally skeptical.

00:48:46.230 --> 00:48:47.113
I would say that—

00:48:47.113 --> 00:48:48.690
It's okay. We're not precious.

00:48:48.690 --> 00:48:49.523
We have lots more ideas.

00:48:49.523 --> 00:48:50.940
No, no, no. It's good.

00:48:50.940 --> 00:48:52.117
There is this question of,

00:48:52.117 --> 00:48:54.990
I think back to our
roots, like, I don't know.

00:48:54.990 --> 00:48:59.990
How do you build a
product that is 10 times better

00:49:00.600 --> 00:49:02.100
than other folks?

00:49:02.100 --> 00:49:03.900
And I think when you get really large,

00:49:03.900 --> 00:49:05.820
actually just a line drive is good

00:49:05.820 --> 00:49:09.060
and a 2x or a 3x is better
kind of thing is actually good.

00:49:09.060 --> 00:49:11.730
But how do you find these sets of things

00:49:11.730 --> 00:49:14.730
that other your product
offering is so different

00:49:14.730 --> 00:49:16.110
than what you can find elsewhere?

00:49:16.110 --> 00:49:17.820
And I think that we can conceivably,

00:49:17.820 --> 00:49:20.460
and I know this, you can negotiate with

00:49:20.460 --> 00:49:22.800
the large logistics companies.

00:49:22.800 --> 00:49:24.360
A deal with FedEx

00:49:24.360 --> 00:49:26.940
that would be better than
what most companies are paying

00:49:26.940 --> 00:49:28.020
and I think that that's interesting.

00:49:28.020 --> 00:49:29.820
Then you have all the sublevels
of how do you get people

00:49:29.820 --> 00:49:31.830
to know about the offer,
to sign in to the offer,

00:49:31.830 --> 00:49:33.390
to deal with all these sub things.

00:49:33.390 --> 00:49:36.840
Or you could just go and
spend that next marginal hour

00:49:36.840 --> 00:49:39.360
to go and say like, let's just automate

00:49:39.360 --> 00:49:41.580
all of accounting for these customers.

00:49:41.580 --> 00:49:46.580
Let's go and start doing financial
work for these customers.

00:49:46.800 --> 00:49:48.660
No financial institution
is able to compete

00:49:48.660 --> 00:49:50.160
on that kind of vector.

00:49:50.160 --> 00:49:51.890
And so, I would say it

00:49:51.890 --> 00:49:54.870
in the fullness of time,
we want to do both.

00:49:54.870 --> 00:49:57.090
Like I think that you want to save people

00:49:57.090 --> 00:49:58.050
the maximum amount of time,

00:49:58.050 --> 00:49:59.850
the maximum amount of money that you can,

00:49:59.850 --> 00:50:01.590
but I think part of what
makes us so different

00:50:01.590 --> 00:50:04.590
in this kind of world that we operate in

00:50:04.590 --> 00:50:07.380
is we have this obsession of

00:50:07.380 --> 00:50:10.860
sources of drag of the things
that slow down purchases

00:50:10.860 --> 00:50:13.860
that lead you to overspend
versus other companies.

00:50:13.860 --> 00:50:15.000
But just to push on that,

00:50:15.000 --> 00:50:18.750
I feel like the
differentiation for companies

00:50:18.750 --> 00:50:21.660
often has to change as time goes on

00:50:21.660 --> 00:50:25.350
because they start in one
competitive equilibrium.

00:50:25.350 --> 00:50:26.820
And then as time goes on,

00:50:26.820 --> 00:50:28.560
they're in another competitive equilibrium

00:50:28.560 --> 00:50:29.580
because it's dynamic.

00:50:29.580 --> 00:50:31.140
That's fair.
And it feels to me

00:50:31.140 --> 00:50:35.520
that Ramp got its start with
very fast product velocity

00:50:35.520 --> 00:50:37.530
and having this great product experience.

00:50:37.530 --> 00:50:39.810
And as you grow up, you
can just build a scale

00:50:39.810 --> 00:50:41.850
into the product and scale…

00:50:41.850 --> 00:50:43.140
Scale-derived product advantages.

00:50:43.140 --> 00:50:44.760
Not just like we're big,

00:50:44.760 --> 00:50:46.590
but like Costco,

00:50:46.590 --> 00:50:48.630
the advantage comes from the scale

00:50:48.630 --> 00:50:50.820
and they really passed
it on to the customer.

00:50:50.820 --> 00:50:52.500
And it feels like there could be

00:50:52.500 --> 00:50:54.000
a second stage to this rocket

00:50:54.000 --> 00:50:56.730
where you get going with
faster product velocity.

00:50:56.730 --> 00:50:59.010
But then there's actually
a pretty different set

00:50:59.010 --> 00:51:03.330
of product differentiations
as you scale up.

00:51:03.330 --> 00:51:04.380
I think it's true.

00:51:04.380 --> 00:51:05.250
I think it's well said

00:51:05.250 --> 00:51:07.830
and there's this almost question of like,

00:51:07.830 --> 00:51:10.770
in every line of business
that we're building,

00:51:10.770 --> 00:51:12.273
where are we in the S-curve?

00:51:13.290 --> 00:51:16.530
How far are we…

00:51:16.530 --> 00:51:18.570
Is this a product that is serving

00:51:18.570 --> 00:51:20.730
these tinkerers, early adopters?

00:51:20.730 --> 00:51:22.800
Are we in the early
majority, are we in the late

00:51:22.800 --> 00:51:25.650
where we need to shift the
business to kind of harvesting

00:51:25.650 --> 00:51:29.310
contribution, optimize
price, not quantity in it

00:51:29.310 --> 00:51:31.050
or towards the end?

00:51:31.050 --> 00:51:33.150
And the thing which is so shocking

00:51:33.150 --> 00:51:37.440
and I think maybe as part
of why I love this business

00:51:37.440 --> 00:51:40.470
and I think even too, I think about Stripe

00:51:40.470 --> 00:51:41.850
and many of the great businesses

00:51:41.850 --> 00:51:44.340
and payments that have been built.

00:51:44.340 --> 00:51:49.340
I believe today Ramp powers
more than 2% of all corporate

00:51:49.530 --> 00:51:52.680
and small business card
transactions in the United States.

00:51:52.680 --> 00:51:55.860
Which is amazing for a business that,

00:51:55.860 --> 00:51:59.490
the product, you couldn't even
sign up for it six years ago.

00:51:59.490 --> 00:52:02.970
And yet that's a really
fancy way of saying

00:52:02.970 --> 00:52:05.170
98% of spend is not on us.

00:52:05.170 --> 00:52:07.110
On Ramp yeah.

00:52:07.110 --> 00:52:09.570
And let's say we do it again.

00:52:09.570 --> 00:52:11.070
We grew faster last year

00:52:11.070 --> 00:52:12.180
than we did the year before.

00:52:12.180 --> 00:52:14.700
I feel very good about

00:52:14.700 --> 00:52:16.740
the way this year is starting off

00:52:16.740 --> 00:52:19.230
and maybe it's closer to four.

00:52:19.230 --> 00:52:21.660
96% is still big.

00:52:21.660 --> 00:52:23.670
92% is still big.

00:52:23.670 --> 00:52:24.660
It is so large.

00:52:24.660 --> 00:52:25.710
And by the way, there's more ways

00:52:25.710 --> 00:52:27.540
to buy things than just cards.

00:52:27.540 --> 00:52:28.710
You think about bill payments.

00:52:28.710 --> 00:52:33.420
And so, I think that
there is an aspect of,

00:52:33.420 --> 00:52:37.830
for most people, there's
so much drudgery around...

00:52:37.830 --> 00:52:40.230
I think people are doing
artisanal expense reports.

00:52:40.230 --> 00:52:41.490
You know, it's fun to be like a hipster

00:52:41.490 --> 00:52:43.080
and spend an hour making coffee,

00:52:43.080 --> 00:52:44.550
but like, it's a little crazy that—

00:52:44.550 --> 00:52:46.260
This is the blue bottle
of expense reports.

00:52:46.260 --> 00:52:48.150
You know but unfortunately
you're in Concur

00:52:48.150 --> 00:52:49.980
and you have like this spinning wheel.

00:52:49.980 --> 00:52:51.480
It's a pour-over expense report.

00:52:51.480 --> 00:52:53.460
It's a pour, we love it that way.

00:52:53.460 --> 00:52:54.480
Single origin.

00:52:54.480 --> 00:52:56.910
You try it. It's the experience.

00:52:56.910 --> 00:52:58.680
You're the Nespresso.

00:52:58.680 --> 00:52:59.910
It's much more efficient.

00:52:59.910 --> 00:53:00.743
That's right.

00:53:00.743 --> 00:53:04.590
But like, what I would
say is this aspect of time

00:53:04.590 --> 00:53:08.040
is most people have not experienced

00:53:08.040 --> 00:53:12.300
this thing you know,

00:53:12.300 --> 00:53:13.920
is just a fact of life

00:53:13.920 --> 00:53:15.780
for building a business that's been hard

00:53:15.780 --> 00:53:17.340
doesn't need to be hard.

00:53:17.340 --> 00:53:18.630
Doing your books doesn't need to be hard.

00:53:18.630 --> 00:53:20.640
And so, I still think in some sense

00:53:20.640 --> 00:53:23.340
in where we are on the
S-curves of our business,

00:53:23.340 --> 00:53:24.420
I lean that way.

00:53:24.420 --> 00:53:27.600
But you're totally right
when you're at this level

00:53:27.600 --> 00:53:30.240
where in the not too distant future…

00:53:30.240 --> 00:53:32.670
I mean, you're there
processing trillions of dollars

00:53:32.670 --> 00:53:35.100
per year in economic activity.

00:53:35.100 --> 00:53:38.739
And we aspire to be there in
the not too distant future.

00:53:38.739 --> 00:53:40.200
Sounds like you'll be there pretty soon.

00:53:40.200 --> 00:53:41.790
We're working our hardest.

00:53:41.790 --> 00:53:42.630
Exponential growth

00:53:42.630 --> 00:53:43.920
is a powerful thing.

00:53:43.920 --> 00:53:46.020
Yes, it's definitely given me good things

00:53:46.020 --> 00:53:47.370
to think about and work towards.

00:53:47.370 --> 00:53:49.650
So I was thinking about when we met,

00:53:49.650 --> 00:53:52.050
when you were running Paribus.

00:53:52.050 --> 00:53:54.570
And it kind of feels like one of the first

00:53:54.570 --> 00:53:57.360
or second or third order effects
of AI is the marginal cost

00:53:57.360 --> 00:54:00.270
of arguing has gone down to zero.

00:54:00.270 --> 00:54:01.380
I'm serious.

00:54:01.380 --> 00:54:02.213
It's true.
It's just like,

00:54:02.213 --> 00:54:05.370
I mean, my wife got into an
argument with some company

00:54:05.370 --> 00:54:07.680
and it's like she used
ChatGPT to argue with them,

00:54:07.680 --> 00:54:09.690
they used it to argue back with her.

00:54:09.690 --> 00:54:11.310
And it's like, I just saw the future.

00:54:11.310 --> 00:54:12.270
This is incredible.

00:54:12.270 --> 00:54:13.103
And like, we won,

00:54:13.103 --> 00:54:13.936
which is great.
Your agent will talk

00:54:13.936 --> 00:54:14.940
to my agent, yeah.

00:54:14.940 --> 00:54:16.110
But you think about this

00:54:16.110 --> 00:54:17.760
from a chargeback where it's like,

00:54:17.760 --> 00:54:19.470
"Hey, this price went down."

00:54:19.470 --> 00:54:21.030
I mean this is how we, I remember like,

00:54:21.030 --> 00:54:22.020
this is such a good idea.

00:54:22.020 --> 00:54:23.250
It's like the price went down,

00:54:23.250 --> 00:54:27.023
you have these breakage models
that the card networks had.

00:54:27.023 --> 00:54:28.890
And Best Buy has to write you a check

00:54:28.890 --> 00:54:30.210
or Visa has to write you a check

00:54:30.210 --> 00:54:31.950
because that TV fell by 20%.

00:54:31.950 --> 00:54:34.493
But nobody does that.

00:54:34.493 --> 00:54:36.510
And now everybody's going to do that.

00:54:36.510 --> 00:54:39.150
Or it's like you do a chargeback for $5,

00:54:39.150 --> 00:54:40.590
it's just not worth it.

00:54:40.590 --> 00:54:41.460
It's like,

00:54:41.460 --> 00:54:43.710
I thought it was Pepsi that
I was buying at McDonald's.

00:54:43.710 --> 00:54:45.600
It was Coke. Chargeback, right?

00:54:45.600 --> 00:54:49.230
Nobody's going to dispute that
because the cost is too high,

00:54:49.230 --> 00:54:50.760
but now the cost goes to zero.

00:54:50.760 --> 00:54:51.720
So I'm just...

00:54:51.720 --> 00:54:54.450
What do you think, I mean, it's hard to,

00:54:54.450 --> 00:54:56.040
picture five years from now

00:54:56.040 --> 00:54:57.780
what the actual full effects of this are.

00:54:57.780 --> 00:54:58.860
But if you kind of think about that

00:54:58.860 --> 00:55:00.690
as the macro abstraction of it's like

00:55:00.690 --> 00:55:01.650
marginal cost of

00:55:01.650 --> 00:55:03.180
arguing goes to zero.

00:55:03.180 --> 00:55:04.290
What changes?

00:55:04.290 --> 00:55:06.000
I think to be really explicit,

00:55:06.000 --> 00:55:08.130
I think what's happening
is the marginal cost

00:55:08.130 --> 00:55:09.337
of time, of knowledge.
Yes.

00:55:09.337 --> 00:55:11.160
That is a much better abstraction.

00:55:11.160 --> 00:55:14.730
Is what's going down so rapidly.

00:55:14.730 --> 00:55:16.710
And I think about our customer base.

00:55:16.710 --> 00:55:19.920
Most of our customers don't
have a single software engineer,

00:55:19.920 --> 00:55:23.100
let alone a software engineer
for their finance team.

00:55:23.100 --> 00:55:26.520
And if what we are very good at doing

00:55:26.520 --> 00:55:31.520
is selling functionally sets of work,

00:55:32.010 --> 00:55:35.130
maybe it's embedded in a
financial operating platform,

00:55:35.130 --> 00:55:37.350
but expenses done, accounting done,

00:55:37.350 --> 00:55:39.540
some type of knowledge work done,

00:55:39.540 --> 00:55:40.530
and you can deliver that.

00:55:40.530 --> 00:55:44.610
That is immense high-leverage
value for these customers.

00:55:44.610 --> 00:55:46.890
Let's say it costs $5 before,

00:55:46.890 --> 00:55:49.170
they didn't do it, now
they can get $5 of value.

00:55:49.170 --> 00:55:53.490
But maybe the cost of…
It's a software type cost,

00:55:53.490 --> 00:55:57.780
maybe it's pennies of tokens
to actually go and do that.

00:55:57.780 --> 00:55:59.280
That is a great business to be in

00:55:59.280 --> 00:56:01.440
because it's very high customer value.

00:56:01.440 --> 00:56:03.270
We can capture just a small amount of that

00:56:03.270 --> 00:56:04.103
and build this business.

00:56:04.103 --> 00:56:05.130
And when you go back to kind of

00:56:05.130 --> 00:56:06.903
the original insight of Ramp,

00:56:08.175 --> 00:56:11.580
we entered into this industry
where it was very profitable,

00:56:11.580 --> 00:56:13.980
but not only was it misaligned,

00:56:13.980 --> 00:56:17.070
but people were fighting
over like basis points.

00:56:17.070 --> 00:56:20.280
Every last dollar that went into rewards

00:56:20.280 --> 00:56:21.840
could have meant like tens or hundreds

00:56:21.840 --> 00:56:25.500
of millions of dollars in
profit for the business,

00:56:25.500 --> 00:56:26.400
for them.

00:56:26.400 --> 00:56:29.853
But if you think about for a customer,

00:56:31.230 --> 00:56:34.560
let's say that in order to
make one extra basis point

00:56:34.560 --> 00:56:36.090
as a business, you
would try to incent them

00:56:36.090 --> 00:56:37.680
to spend a hundred more dollars.

00:56:37.680 --> 00:56:40.020
For that customer and let's
say they buy something

00:56:40.020 --> 00:56:41.370
that gym membership they didn't need,

00:56:41.370 --> 00:56:43.590
or that subscription keeps going,

00:56:43.590 --> 00:56:45.720
they have lost $100,
it's gone out the door

00:56:45.720 --> 00:56:48.000
and maybe they go to the
gym, maybe they don't.

00:56:48.000 --> 00:56:50.340
But that is out of their bank account.

00:56:50.340 --> 00:56:51.786
And if you just try to say,

00:56:51.786 --> 00:56:54.120
I'm going to have a
better rewards program,

00:56:54.120 --> 00:56:56.280
sure, maybe you can get that customer

00:56:56.280 --> 00:56:59.610
a dollar or $1.10 back or some amount.

00:56:59.610 --> 00:57:02.730
That pales in comparison
to helping them just

00:57:02.730 --> 00:57:05.550
not spend that $100,
cancel the subscription.

00:57:05.550 --> 00:57:08.250
The economic leverage of
that activity is much higher.

00:57:08.250 --> 00:57:11.430
You sold your last business
Paribus to Capital One.

00:57:11.430 --> 00:57:15.270
Capital One is one of the biggest

00:57:15.270 --> 00:57:17.760
founder-run financial firms

00:57:17.760 --> 00:57:20.310
that people in Silicon
Valley don't talk about.

00:57:20.310 --> 00:57:21.240
What should we all be...

00:57:21.240 --> 00:57:23.850
And has been extraordinarily successful.

00:57:23.850 --> 00:57:26.030
What should we all be
learning from Capital One

00:57:26.030 --> 00:57:26.937
in their success?

00:57:26.937 --> 00:57:28.860
Actually, I should say, how did they?

00:57:28.860 --> 00:57:31.290
What product really broke out for them?

00:57:31.290 --> 00:57:33.273
Just what is Capital One success?

00:57:34.140 --> 00:57:35.370
I think there's a lot

00:57:35.370 --> 00:57:37.650
that makes them amazing as a company.

00:57:37.650 --> 00:57:39.750
Both the founders are excellent.

00:57:39.750 --> 00:57:42.300
Rich Fairbank, Nigel Morris.

00:57:42.300 --> 00:57:43.133
I think it's worth people
reading up on their stories.

00:57:43.133 --> 00:57:43.966
It was founded in '90s,

00:57:47.340 --> 00:57:48.173
is that right?

00:57:48.173 --> 00:57:51.623
So, not quite.

00:57:51.623 --> 00:57:52.984
From a legal standpoint—

00:57:52.984 --> 00:57:54.570
I'm very wooly on my Capital One history.

00:57:54.570 --> 00:57:57.360
I think that legally the
incorporation in some sense

00:57:57.360 --> 00:58:01.440
for Capital One, it was,
I think it was in 1994,

00:58:01.440 --> 00:58:05.183
but the actual start
traces back to the '80s.

00:58:05.183 --> 00:58:10.080
At the time Rich and Nigel
had met, they were consultants

00:58:10.080 --> 00:58:13.090
and they had this insight that

00:58:14.700 --> 00:58:17.883
the business of credit cards was…

00:58:18.870 --> 00:58:22.980
While it was lucrative, was not
serving most of the country.

00:58:22.980 --> 00:58:24.270
The way you could kind of think about

00:58:24.270 --> 00:58:26.700
credit cards back then, you know

00:58:26.700 --> 00:58:28.350
Diners Club, maybe
others have heard of it.

00:58:28.350 --> 00:58:31.410
It was was a way for rich business people

00:58:31.410 --> 00:58:32.910
to get together and have lunch.

00:58:32.910 --> 00:58:34.770
And like they put the card down

00:58:34.770 --> 00:58:37.770
and the restaurant would pick up the tab

00:58:37.770 --> 00:58:41.130
and they could go and pay
the restaurant back later.

00:58:41.130 --> 00:58:42.630
And they had kind of navigated that

00:58:42.630 --> 00:58:45.240
and the simplification is

00:58:45.240 --> 00:58:47.820
if you had a very high credit score,

00:58:47.820 --> 00:58:49.320
you could get one of these cards

00:58:49.320 --> 00:58:51.000
with the benefits that it had.

00:58:51.000 --> 00:58:54.270
And if you didn't have a
credit score that met that,

00:58:54.270 --> 00:58:55.590
you could have a debit card.

00:58:55.590 --> 00:58:56.423
And that was basically it.

00:58:56.423 --> 00:58:59.490
It was a very linear cutoff.

00:58:59.490 --> 00:59:03.780
And what Rich and Nigel… The
insight that they had had

00:59:03.780 --> 00:59:06.363
is there must be some curve.

00:59:07.260 --> 00:59:08.640
We should be able to test this.

00:59:08.640 --> 00:59:10.680
Maybe we give people cards that have

00:59:10.680 --> 00:59:12.930
above an 800 credit score
or something like that then.

00:59:12.930 --> 00:59:15.180
But what about 790?

00:59:15.180 --> 00:59:18.120
There might be people there
that can go and pay for this.

00:59:18.120 --> 00:59:21.720
If and the way we could go
and take on the cost of this,

00:59:21.720 --> 00:59:24.180
maybe we charge them a
somewhat higher interest rate

00:59:24.180 --> 00:59:26.605
until they prove their efficacy.

00:59:26.605 --> 00:59:28.530
If 790 works, let's go to 780.

00:59:28.530 --> 00:59:30.300
So it's kind of the BNPL of its day.

00:59:30.300 --> 00:59:32.700
There is a population who
are, for whatever reason,

00:59:32.700 --> 00:59:34.710
just below the threshold where banks

00:59:34.710 --> 00:59:36.810
are giving them good access to credit

00:59:36.810 --> 00:59:38.880
where you can actually very
profitably lend to them.

00:59:38.880 --> 00:59:40.673
This is right. Exactly.

00:59:40.673 --> 00:59:44.190
And so, they, in the '80s,
were functionally pitching

00:59:44.190 --> 00:59:46.417
all these different banks to say,

00:59:46.417 --> 00:59:48.933
"We should go on this exploration.

00:59:50.940 --> 00:59:52.290
We'll even run this for you.

00:59:52.290 --> 00:59:53.310
Let us go and run this."

00:59:53.310 --> 00:59:55.560
And they went door to door
to door to door to door—

00:59:55.560 --> 00:59:56.790
The banks didn't buy it.

00:59:56.790 --> 00:59:57.623
They didn't buy it.

00:59:57.623 --> 01:00:01.290
And then finally, there was a
bank in Virginia, Signet Bank,

01:00:01.290 --> 01:00:03.480
that said, "Fine, we'll let you run it.

01:00:03.480 --> 01:00:05.160
You can come join.

01:00:05.160 --> 01:00:07.050
We'll give you this group

01:00:07.050 --> 01:00:08.610
and some resources to go build this."

01:00:08.610 --> 01:00:11.927
So they built this as this
division inside of it.

01:00:11.927 --> 01:00:13.650
And it started to work.

01:00:13.650 --> 01:00:14.730
As this was going on,

01:00:14.730 --> 01:00:17.160
the computer revolution was taking off

01:00:17.160 --> 01:00:20.340
and it was sort of an unfortunate acronym,

01:00:20.340 --> 01:00:23.827
but they called it IBS.

01:00:23.827 --> 01:00:26.543
Irritable something something.

01:00:26.543 --> 01:00:28.503
No, it's Information-Based Strategy.

01:00:29.520 --> 01:00:31.950
And they said, "We could use
different pockets of data

01:00:31.950 --> 01:00:33.600
and run these."

01:00:33.600 --> 01:00:35.730
It was before there was big data,

01:00:35.730 --> 01:00:38.910
there was this kind of data
of we could go and test.

01:00:38.910 --> 01:00:40.710
You know, maybe this person has a balance

01:00:40.710 --> 01:00:42.300
of $8,000 at this bank.

01:00:42.300 --> 01:00:44.880
We could send them an
offer at this interest rate

01:00:44.880 --> 01:00:46.387
or this kind of

01:00:46.387 --> 01:00:48.150
you don't need to pay back interest

01:00:48.150 --> 01:00:49.440
for six months or 12 months.

01:00:49.440 --> 01:00:50.370
And they could start to go

01:00:50.370 --> 01:00:53.550
and see the mathematical
return against this.

01:00:53.550 --> 01:00:55.800
And this division started really working

01:00:55.800 --> 01:00:57.780
and it got so profitable

01:00:57.780 --> 01:00:59.910
it became a problem for the business

01:00:59.910 --> 01:01:01.500
and they convinced them to spin it out.

01:01:01.500 --> 01:01:06.203
And so in 1994, that is
the founding as people know

01:01:06.203 --> 01:01:07.290
Capital One today.

01:01:07.290 --> 01:01:10.800
But it actually was I
think almost a decade

01:01:10.800 --> 01:01:13.860
in building to actually go and do this.

01:01:13.860 --> 01:01:16.920
I think that there was a lot
that they got really right.

01:01:16.920 --> 01:01:19.230
One, I think that they took much more

01:01:19.230 --> 01:01:22.770
of a first-principles
view of the business.

01:01:22.770 --> 01:01:25.410
Whereas others said, "This
is a profitable product.

01:01:25.410 --> 01:01:26.670
I'm going to do it like the other person

01:01:26.670 --> 01:01:28.740
and use scale and
distribution advantages."

01:01:28.740 --> 01:01:29.850
They said, "Well, let's think about

01:01:29.850 --> 01:01:31.200
the product nature itself."

01:01:31.200 --> 01:01:33.090
There's different, everyone's
making money on interchange.

01:01:33.090 --> 01:01:35.250
Maybe there's lending could work

01:01:35.250 --> 01:01:37.440
and you could kind of
have different strategies

01:01:37.440 --> 01:01:39.030
for different types of populations.

01:01:39.030 --> 01:01:40.320
They did this so large

01:01:40.320 --> 01:01:42.720
where I think at one point
they were the largest customer

01:01:42.720 --> 01:01:44.670
of the US Postal Service.

01:01:44.670 --> 01:01:45.870
They would send out so many offers

01:01:45.870 --> 01:01:47.850
I think until Amazon took them over.

01:01:47.850 --> 01:01:49.440
There was a period of
time that they did this.

01:01:49.440 --> 01:01:52.295
So it's incredibly experimental.

01:01:52.295 --> 01:01:53.730
They kind of carved the path

01:01:53.730 --> 01:01:55.680
and showed how do you go
from credit facilities

01:01:55.680 --> 01:01:57.930
for fintech founders,

01:01:57.930 --> 01:01:59.460
eventually maybe you become a bank

01:01:59.460 --> 01:02:01.170
or buy a bank and carve that path.

01:02:01.170 --> 01:02:02.850
So there's some tactical lessons.

01:02:02.850 --> 01:02:04.260
I also think they've done a great job

01:02:04.260 --> 01:02:06.630
of building a great and durable brand.

01:02:06.630 --> 01:02:09.570
One of the things that Capital
One has done very, very well

01:02:09.570 --> 01:02:10.890
I think has focused on like,

01:02:10.890 --> 01:02:13.890
what is the consistent visual message?

01:02:13.890 --> 01:02:16.680
What is the aesthetic
and how do you stand out

01:02:16.680 --> 01:02:19.167
in this busy and different world?

01:02:19.167 --> 01:02:21.780
And so, there's a lot of pieces

01:02:21.780 --> 01:02:23.460
to what's made that company work, but—

01:02:23.460 --> 01:02:25.410
And they've also remained focused,

01:02:25.410 --> 01:02:27.300
which a lot of banks haven't.

01:02:27.300 --> 01:02:28.380
I would say so.

01:02:28.380 --> 01:02:32.817
Well, I think that is
a rewrite of history.

01:02:33.810 --> 01:02:37.110
They were doing like cell-phone financing,

01:02:37.110 --> 01:02:38.460
healthcare financing.

01:02:38.460 --> 01:02:39.338
They were a part of this thing.

01:02:39.338 --> 01:02:41.370
They have come back to focus
after wandering in the woods.

01:02:41.370 --> 01:02:44.220
They had some JV, it was like America One,

01:02:44.220 --> 01:02:46.560
I think it was called,
to compete with Amazon.

01:02:46.560 --> 01:02:50.610
They were actually the most experimental

01:02:50.610 --> 01:02:53.880
business ever in a lot of ways.

01:02:53.880 --> 01:02:58.620
In, I think it was
Hibernia Bank in Louisiana.

01:02:58.620 --> 01:03:00.660
And this was after Hurricane Katrina.

01:03:00.660 --> 01:03:01.950
I think it was maybe during it

01:03:01.950 --> 01:03:03.060
or just after they were able to buy.

01:03:03.060 --> 01:03:05.487
And it was like, it was an
existential question for them.

01:03:05.487 --> 01:03:08.400
And I think that many
people worried about,

01:03:08.400 --> 01:03:10.920
you knew this like, well
too from the BNPL businesses

01:03:10.920 --> 01:03:14.430
where if you're kind of
a lending-based business,

01:03:14.430 --> 01:03:17.700
and it's fine when interest rates are low,

01:03:17.700 --> 01:03:18.780
but if there's a crisis

01:03:18.780 --> 01:03:20.130
and people don't want to lend to you,

01:03:20.130 --> 01:03:22.350
it can just break your business.

01:03:22.350 --> 01:03:24.870
And so they had these kind of scares

01:03:24.870 --> 01:03:26.910
and they said we need a
stable kind of cost base.

01:03:26.910 --> 01:03:27.870
Eventually they bought a bank,

01:03:27.870 --> 01:03:29.610
which was in a lot of ways very good.

01:03:29.610 --> 01:03:32.700
They had kind of the deposits
as this durable, stable,

01:03:32.700 --> 01:03:36.330
low cost of, or consistent capital base.

01:03:36.330 --> 01:03:39.510
But the flip side of buying it

01:03:39.510 --> 01:03:43.380
was they had to reconcile their culture

01:03:43.380 --> 01:03:47.370
of like crazy experimentation
of trying everything to

01:03:47.370 --> 01:03:49.110
we're a regulated bank now.

01:03:49.110 --> 01:03:50.760
We got to be bank people.

01:03:50.760 --> 01:03:53.010
And it's more than just an attitude thing.

01:03:53.010 --> 01:03:55.620
If you are a nationally chartered bank—

01:03:55.620 --> 01:03:57.420
It's real strictures, yeah.

01:03:57.420 --> 01:04:01.350
You know, look, it's to a level of,

01:04:01.350 --> 01:04:04.383
let's say the bank fails on Monday night.

01:04:05.880 --> 01:04:08.220
There is personnel.

01:04:08.220 --> 01:04:10.716
These guys in windbreakers
in your office the next day.

01:04:10.716 --> 01:04:11.880
And they've probably been working there

01:04:11.880 --> 01:04:13.320
for some time before.

01:04:13.320 --> 01:04:15.420
You know, and so it's...

01:04:15.420 --> 01:04:17.583
I think there was a change.

01:04:18.870 --> 01:04:21.090
In some sense, there is experimentation

01:04:21.090 --> 01:04:24.630
I think within certain constraints

01:04:24.630 --> 01:04:26.820
would be how I would describe

01:04:26.820 --> 01:04:30.900
the Capital One post buying
a bank versus before

01:04:30.900 --> 01:04:32.340
would be my read of it.

01:04:32.340 --> 01:04:34.860
But when you look at them
in the '90s and 2000s,

01:04:34.860 --> 01:04:37.710
it was like them and in some ways,

01:04:37.710 --> 01:04:39.840
like name the high flying tech company.

01:04:39.840 --> 01:04:44.003
They were right up there in
terms of share price growth.

01:04:44.003 --> 01:04:46.410
They started re-achieving it
I think in the late 2010s.

01:04:46.410 --> 01:04:49.283
But it's a fascinating company.

01:04:49.283 --> 01:04:50.340
I mean, I think the talent pool.

01:04:50.340 --> 01:04:54.450
So at Affirm, our first like
really good risk person,

01:04:54.450 --> 01:04:56.070
but this is true for
every fintech company.

01:04:56.070 --> 01:04:57.630
It's just like, actually
this is kind of cool.

01:04:57.630 --> 01:04:59.970
Billy Alvarado, our first
COO grew up at Capital One.

01:04:59.970 --> 01:05:01.170
I had played the piano

01:05:01.170 --> 01:05:03.510
and my teacher was taught by somebody

01:05:03.510 --> 01:05:04.343
who was taught by somebody.

01:05:04.343 --> 01:05:05.880
There was actually a chart,
you can look this up.

01:05:05.880 --> 01:05:08.940
Czerny and Beethoven taught everybody

01:05:08.940 --> 01:05:10.530
who ever taught anybody.

01:05:10.530 --> 01:05:12.900
So like Lang Lang or Yuja Wang,

01:05:12.900 --> 01:05:14.467
like pick any famous pianist today.

01:05:14.467 --> 01:05:16.020
They can always

01:05:16.020 --> 01:05:16.920
trace their lineage

01:05:16.920 --> 01:05:21.920
100% of the time to Czerny,
Carl Czerny or Beethoven.

01:05:22.523 --> 01:05:23.356
Capital One is like the Czerny.

01:05:23.356 --> 01:05:27.423
Our Head of Risk Srinath
Srinivasan, he's amazing,

01:05:29.370 --> 01:05:30.203
came from Capital.

01:05:30.203 --> 01:05:33.030
We had the same boss
10 years apart actually,

01:05:33.030 --> 01:05:34.200
in some sense.

01:05:34.200 --> 01:05:38.040
And all the heads of risk
for all modern fintechs

01:05:38.040 --> 01:05:39.180
came from Capital One.

01:05:39.180 --> 01:05:40.830
Well, because it's one
of these things where

01:05:40.830 --> 01:05:43.230
you know that you don't want
to just hire for intercept,

01:05:43.230 --> 01:05:44.180
you want to hire for slope.

01:05:44.180 --> 01:05:46.170
And this is the problem is like you find,

01:05:46.170 --> 01:05:48.120
not to pick on Bank of America

01:05:48.120 --> 01:05:49.860
or Chase or somebody,
but it's like, all right,

01:05:49.860 --> 01:05:52.160
this person clearly knows
how to do their job.

01:05:53.040 --> 01:05:55.440
The bank that they work in
is worth a lot of money.

01:05:55.440 --> 01:05:57.390
But they don't necessarily have,

01:05:57.390 --> 01:05:59.280
how do I put this delicately, the slope.

01:05:59.280 --> 01:06:00.113
They won't know

01:06:00.113 --> 01:06:01.080
what that means because they don't know

01:06:01.080 --> 01:06:01.913
what slope means.

01:06:01.913 --> 01:06:03.690
But seriously, the guy that we hired

01:06:03.690 --> 01:06:06.420
just so… He was very, very smart.

01:06:06.420 --> 01:06:08.220
And that was the key
thing about Capital One

01:06:08.220 --> 01:06:10.740
is that they didn't hire banking people,

01:06:10.740 --> 01:06:12.360
they just hired smart people.

01:06:12.360 --> 01:06:14.640
And it became known as the
place where smart people went.

01:06:14.640 --> 01:06:17.850
And actually the fact that
companies would poach people

01:06:17.850 --> 01:06:20.130
from Capital One actually
added to the alert.

01:06:20.130 --> 01:06:22.080
It's like, I want to work at McKinsey

01:06:22.080 --> 01:06:23.700
because at the end of McKinsey,

01:06:23.700 --> 01:06:25.031
I'm going to get a better job
somewhere else.

01:06:25.031 --> 01:06:25.864
Capital One job market.

01:06:25.864 --> 01:06:27.660
Capital One actually had,

01:06:27.660 --> 01:06:28.980
it did have that imprimatur.

01:06:28.980 --> 01:06:29.850
And I think part of it is

01:06:29.850 --> 01:06:32.520
it's the only founder-led institution.

01:06:32.520 --> 01:06:33.480
Last question, Eric.

01:06:33.480 --> 01:06:34.560
We're talking about banking here.

01:06:34.560 --> 01:06:35.910
You have a treasury product.

01:06:35.910 --> 01:06:36.780
In five years,

01:06:36.780 --> 01:06:38.340
where do businesses keep their money?

01:06:38.340 --> 01:06:40.590
Is it with, I mean statistically mostly,

01:06:40.590 --> 01:06:42.090
you mentioned 2% on Ramp,

01:06:42.090 --> 01:06:45.240
it's even higher today
in traditional banks

01:06:45.240 --> 01:06:46.890
like a Chase or a Bank of America.

01:06:46.890 --> 01:06:50.073
There's also neobanks, you know,

01:06:52.067 --> 01:06:54.750
I mean kind of bank-like
entities like Mercury

01:06:54.750 --> 01:06:58.440
or Revolut or Monzo or things like that.

01:06:58.440 --> 01:07:00.600
There are companies like Ramp

01:07:00.600 --> 01:07:02.190
where you started in spend cards,

01:07:02.190 --> 01:07:03.930
but maybe moving more into treasury.

01:07:03.930 --> 01:07:08.250
How do you think that
shakes out as a market?

01:07:08.250 --> 01:07:11.160
So, as a macro point,

01:07:11.160 --> 01:07:14.220
I think there is an
incredible amount of money

01:07:14.220 --> 01:07:19.140
made by institutions who
are enjoying the profit pool

01:07:19.140 --> 01:07:22.680
and sharing very little
with their end customers.

01:07:22.680 --> 01:07:24.360
You know, if you think
about the implications

01:07:24.360 --> 01:07:26.940
of like the federal overnight funds rate.

01:07:26.940 --> 01:07:28.650
This is basically saying,

01:07:28.650 --> 01:07:30.003
hey, if you want to go and—

01:07:30.003 --> 01:07:32.160
Just it's insane that there's
so little yield sharing

01:07:32.160 --> 01:07:32.993
in the current market.

01:07:32.993 --> 01:07:33.826
It's crazy.

01:07:33.826 --> 01:07:36.240
I think that the national
average for businesses, right,

01:07:36.240 --> 01:07:39.690
these are sophisticated
entities with personnel.

01:07:39.690 --> 01:07:40.740
They're supposed to be able to manage

01:07:40.740 --> 01:07:43.680
and put their funds in some
place that's more high yield.

01:07:43.680 --> 01:07:46.050
I think the national average

01:07:46.050 --> 01:07:49.697
on checking accounts is 0.07% in the US.

01:07:49.697 --> 01:07:51.210
And so I'm guessing you don't believe

01:07:51.210 --> 01:07:53.580
that Bank of America and JP
Morgan and all these folks

01:07:53.580 --> 01:07:56.160
will wake up more altruistic one day.

01:07:56.160 --> 01:07:59.250
And so, what is the competitive process

01:07:59.250 --> 01:08:00.840
by which you think we'll get there?

01:08:00.840 --> 01:08:04.320
I think that new businesses,

01:08:04.320 --> 01:08:05.850
whether it's they have their own charter

01:08:05.850 --> 01:08:08.256
or they work with banks too that,

01:08:08.256 --> 01:08:10.530
this is not a monopoly.

01:08:10.530 --> 01:08:13.230
Like they have, everyone has competitors

01:08:13.230 --> 01:08:14.670
and it is a market that evolves.

01:08:14.670 --> 01:08:17.220
I think that that rate will go up,

01:08:17.220 --> 01:08:19.410
that the easier it is for people to,

01:08:19.410 --> 01:08:23.310
whether it's to create
depository institutions,

01:08:23.310 --> 01:08:25.380
create accounts at these institutions,

01:08:25.380 --> 01:08:27.600
or create stores of value

01:08:27.600 --> 01:08:29.310
maybe even outside of these systems,

01:08:29.310 --> 01:08:31.080
maybe in a stable currency.

01:08:31.080 --> 01:08:32.790
And so, I think that rate goes up.

01:08:32.790 --> 01:08:36.210
I think part of what's driven
the extremely rapid growth

01:08:36.210 --> 01:08:38.010
of Ramp Treasury is it's just

01:08:38.010 --> 01:08:41.100
a vastly better deal for customers.

01:08:41.100 --> 01:08:43.950
Maybe you keep three months

01:08:43.950 --> 01:08:47.070
of just walking around money
in your checking account,

01:08:47.070 --> 01:08:49.560
but if you know the funds
you're going to receive

01:08:49.560 --> 01:08:51.420
and what you're paying out,

01:08:51.420 --> 01:08:54.600
well, we can move funds on the day payroll

01:08:54.600 --> 01:08:57.420
is coming due into that account

01:08:57.420 --> 01:08:59.850
and then every other day make
sure the funds are earning

01:08:59.850 --> 01:09:01.020
the highest rate possible.

01:09:01.020 --> 01:09:03.060
And so, I think from a macro perspective,

01:09:03.060 --> 01:09:05.940
these things tend to go up in
terms of the relative yield.

01:09:05.940 --> 01:09:10.200
I think there's another
question of slack in the system.

01:09:10.200 --> 01:09:13.380
If you have more and more...

01:09:13.380 --> 01:09:15.900
Money can think, right?

01:09:15.900 --> 01:09:18.390
If the dollars in your company

01:09:18.390 --> 01:09:21.930
have some level of intelligence

01:09:21.930 --> 01:09:26.670
like it's able to kind of determine

01:09:26.670 --> 01:09:29.583
when can it be spent,
under what circumstances?

01:09:29.583 --> 01:09:33.420
It's increasingly recorded in real time

01:09:33.420 --> 01:09:35.550
and there's some reasoning around this,

01:09:35.550 --> 01:09:36.930
some ability to kind of opine

01:09:36.930 --> 01:09:39.750
of where should the
next marginal dollar go?

01:09:39.750 --> 01:09:41.100
And you have systems that are able

01:09:41.100 --> 01:09:43.800
to think infinitely about these things

01:09:43.800 --> 01:09:44.910
even at 3:00 AM in the morning

01:09:44.910 --> 01:09:46.560
when most of your team is asleep.

01:09:47.640 --> 01:09:49.260
Well, once you determine,

01:09:49.260 --> 01:09:51.960
like maybe you'll determine
I should keep the funds

01:09:51.960 --> 01:09:55.593
in some level in like a 2%
or a 4% yielding account.

01:09:56.550 --> 01:09:59.580
But I think more of those
dollars will go in flight

01:09:59.580 --> 01:10:01.320
actually to go spend to,
if you have a business

01:10:01.320 --> 01:10:03.630
that makes an 8% profit margin a year,

01:10:03.630 --> 01:10:05.790
that's a lot higher than what you can earn

01:10:05.790 --> 01:10:06.780
in the overnight rate.

01:10:06.780 --> 01:10:08.400
And so in some sense, I think
you have the dual effect.

01:10:08.400 --> 01:10:09.840
Smarter capital allocation.

01:10:09.840 --> 01:10:11.610
I think more dollars will be put to work.

01:10:11.610 --> 01:10:15.030
I agree with Alex's macro point of

01:10:15.030 --> 01:10:17.640
if you kind of understand counterparties,

01:10:17.640 --> 01:10:18.810
you understand more information.

01:10:18.810 --> 01:10:21.840
I think that one, the cost
of financing should go down

01:10:21.840 --> 01:10:23.280
and I think more dollars

01:10:23.280 --> 01:10:25.050
should be in the system in some sense.

01:10:25.050 --> 01:10:26.910
It's actually a waste for everyone

01:10:26.910 --> 01:10:28.950
to have your dollars just
sitting in a bank account.

01:10:28.950 --> 01:10:29.940
Who does that benefit?

01:10:29.940 --> 01:10:30.907
But it goes back to your time point.

01:10:30.907 --> 01:10:33.720
And it's like the reason why

01:10:33.720 --> 01:10:35.700
I got mad at Chase a while ago,

01:10:35.700 --> 01:10:36.960
and I still have a Chase account.

01:10:36.960 --> 01:10:37.860
And like why is that?

01:10:37.860 --> 01:10:39.600
It's like I have a life insurance policy.

01:10:39.600 --> 01:10:40.750
I don't remember the login for it.

01:10:40.750 --> 01:10:42.450
It's just too much work.

01:10:42.450 --> 01:10:43.560
I wrote, this is a true story.

01:10:43.560 --> 01:10:44.760
Isn't this an issue if you die?

01:10:44.760 --> 01:10:45.593
Yeah, I mean.

01:10:45.593 --> 01:10:46.530
Well, but they'll pay my wife.

01:10:46.530 --> 01:10:47.363
I just don't know

01:10:47.363 --> 01:10:49.170
how to log in and change the bank account.

01:10:49.170 --> 01:10:52.070
So, they'll send her a nice
letter I'm sure, some flowers.

01:10:53.370 --> 01:10:55.020
But, you know, why...

01:10:55.020 --> 01:10:56.070
Well actually, this is a true story.

01:10:56.070 --> 01:10:58.800
I wrote a check for
somebody's bar mitzvah.

01:10:58.800 --> 01:10:59.790
Mazel tov.

01:10:59.790 --> 01:11:01.350
Has not been deposited.

01:11:01.350 --> 01:11:04.451
Do I want to be the schmuck
who has the bounced check?

01:11:04.451 --> 01:11:06.810
No, I don't.

01:11:06.810 --> 01:11:07.643
So it's like I have to—

01:11:07.643 --> 01:11:09.610
This kid is keeping you at Chase.

01:11:09.610 --> 01:11:10.860
Well, great to see you.

01:11:10.860 --> 01:11:12.090
You too.
Eric, thank you.

01:11:12.090 --> 01:11:13.320
Thanks for the Guinness.

01:11:13.320 --> 01:11:16.951
It was a great time.

